// --- Lesson 1: Changes During the Industrial Revolution in Britain ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Was the Industrial Revolution?\n\nThe **Industrial Revolution** was a period of major change in the way goods were produced. It began in **Britain** in the late 1700s and continued into the 1800s. Before the Industrial Revolution, most goods were made by hand in homes or small workshops. Afterwards, goods were produced in large **factories** using machines.\n\n### Why Did It Start in Britain?\n\n| Factor | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Raw materials** | Britain had large supplies of coal and iron ore |\n| **Capital (money)** | Profits from the slave trade and colonialism funded new industries |\n| **Labour** | Enclosure of common land pushed rural workers into cities |\n| **Transport** | Good river and canal systems; later railways |\n| **Technology** | Inventions like the spinning jenny, water frame, and steam engine |\n| **Empire** | Colonies provided raw materials and markets for finished goods |\n\n### Key Industries\n\n- **Textiles**: The cotton industry was the first to be mechanised. Raw cotton came from slave plantations in America and was turned into cloth in British factories.\n- **Iron and steel**: Used for machines, railways, bridges, and buildings.\n- **Coal mining**: Coal powered the steam engines that drove factories and railways.'),
  q(2, 'Why is the slave trade relevant to the start of the Industrial Revolution in Britain?',
    ['Profits from the slave trade provided capital to fund new industries and technology', 'Slaves worked in British factories', 'The slave trade ended the Industrial Revolution', 'The slave trade had nothing to do with British industry'], 0,
    'The transatlantic slave trade generated enormous profits for British merchants and investors. This wealth (capital) was invested in new factories, machines, and infrastructure, helping to fund the Industrial Revolution.'),
  t(3, '## The Economy Before the Industrial Revolution\n\nBefore the Industrial Revolution, Britain had a largely **agricultural economy**. Most people lived in the countryside and made a living through farming.\n\n### The Domestic System (Cottage Industry)\n\n- Workers produced goods (especially textiles) in their own **homes** using simple tools and hand-powered machines\n- Merchants brought raw materials to workers and collected the finished products\n- Work was slow but flexible — families could set their own pace\n- The entire family contributed, including children\n\n### The Enclosure Movement\n\n- From the 1700s, wealthy landowners **enclosed** (fenced off) common land that had previously been shared by villagers\n- Small farmers lost access to land for grazing animals and growing crops\n- Many were forced to move to **towns and cities** to find work in the new factories\n- This created a large pool of cheap labour for factory owners\n\n### Types of Revolutions\n\nThe term "revolution" means a fundamental change:\n- **Agricultural revolution**: New farming methods (crop rotation, selective breeding, seed drill) increased food production\n- **Industrial Revolution**: The shift from hand production to machine production in factories\n- Both revolutions transformed British society and, eventually, the world.'),
  fb(4, 'Before the Industrial Revolution, workers produced goods in their homes in a system called the ___ system. The ___ movement forced small farmers off common land and into cities.',
    ['domestic', 'enclosure'],
    'The domestic (or cottage industry) system involved workers making goods at home. The enclosure movement pushed people off communal land, creating the workforce that would fill the new factories.'),
  t(5, '## Social Changes During the Industrial Revolution\n\nThe Industrial Revolution transformed the lives of millions of people — but not always for the better.\n\n### Urbanisation\n\n- People flocked from the countryside to cities like Manchester, Birmingham, Leeds, and London in search of factory work\n- Cities grew rapidly and without planning\n- **Living conditions** in working-class areas were terrible:\n  - Overcrowded, damp, and dirty housing\n  - No proper sewerage or clean water\n  - Disease spread quickly — cholera, typhoid, and tuberculosis were common\n  - Average life expectancy in industrial cities was as low as **25-30 years**\n\n### Factory Conditions\n\n- Workers — including **women and children** — worked **12-16 hours a day**, six days a week\n- Factories were dangerous: unguarded machinery caused horrific injuries\n- The air was thick with dust and fumes\n- Workers could be beaten or fined for being late, talking, or falling asleep\n- **Child labour** was widespread — children as young as 5 worked in mines and factories\n\n### Child Labour\n\n| Workplace | What Children Did |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Cotton mills** | Crawled under machines to collect loose cotton; small fingers tied broken threads |\n| **Coal mines** | Pulled coal carts through narrow tunnels ("trappers" opened ventilation doors) |\n| **Chimneys** | Climbed inside chimneys to clean them ("climbing boys") |\n| **Workshops** | Long hours for very low pay in cramped conditions |'),
  q(6, 'Why were children widely employed in factories and mines during the Industrial Revolution?',
    ['They were cheap to employ, their small bodies could fit into tight spaces, and there were no laws to protect them', 'Children enjoyed working in factories', 'There were no adults available for work', 'The government required children to work'], 0,
    'Factory and mine owners employed children because they could pay them very little, their small size allowed them to do tasks adults could not (like crawling under machines or through mine tunnels), and initially there were no laws preventing child labour.'),
  t(7, '## Working-Class Resistance\n\nWorkers did not accept their conditions passively. They organised and fought for their rights.\n\n### Trade Unions\n\n- Workers began forming **trade unions** — organisations to fight for better wages and working conditions\n- Early unions were **illegal** — the Combination Acts (1799-1800) banned workers from organising\n- Despite this, workers held secret meetings and went on **strikes** (refusing to work)\n- The Combination Acts were repealed in 1824, allowing unions to form legally\n- Unions used **collective bargaining** — negotiating as a group rather than as individuals\n\n### The Luddites\n\n- Some workers smashed the new machines that they believed were taking their jobs\n- These machine-breakers were called **Luddites** (named after "Ned Ludd")\n- The government sent soldiers to stop them and made machine-breaking punishable by **death**\n\n### Labour Resistance and Power\n\n- Through strikes and pressure, workers gradually won improvements:\n  - **Factory Act (1833)**: No children under 9 in textile factories; children 9-13 limited to 8 hours a day\n  - **Mines Act (1842)**: No women or children under 10 underground in mines\n  - **Ten Hours Act (1847)**: Limited working day to 10 hours for women and children\n- These reforms were won through decades of struggle, not given willingly by factory owners'),
  fb(8, 'Workers who smashed machines because they feared losing their jobs were called ___. Workers organised into trade ___ to fight for better wages and conditions.',
    ['Luddites', 'unions'],
    'The Luddites were skilled textile workers who destroyed machines they saw as threatening their livelihoods. Trade unions gave workers collective power to negotiate with employers and eventually helped win important legal protections.'),
  q(9, 'What did the Factory Act of 1833 achieve?',
    ['It banned children under 9 from textile factories and limited working hours for older children', 'It closed all factories in Britain', 'It gave workers the right to vote', 'It increased working hours for all workers'], 0,
    'The Factory Act of 1833 was one of the first laws to protect child workers. It banned children under 9 from working in textile factories and limited children aged 9-13 to 8 hours per day. Factory inspectors were appointed to enforce the law.'),
  t(10, '## Increased Power and Wealth\n\nThe Industrial Revolution made Britain the wealthiest and most powerful nation in the world by the mid-1800s.\n\n### The Growth of Empire\n\n- Factories needed **raw materials** (cotton, rubber, metals) from around the world\n- Finished goods needed **markets** to sell to\n- This drove the expansion of the **British Empire** — by 1900 it covered about a quarter of the world\n- Colonies in Africa, India, and Asia provided cheap raw materials and captive markets\n\n### The New Social Classes\n\nThe Industrial Revolution created new social divisions:\n\n| Class | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Upper class** | Aristocracy and wealthy factory owners; lived in luxury |\n| **Middle class** | Factory managers, merchants, professionals; growing in size and influence |\n| **Working class** | Factory workers, miners, labourers; the majority of the population; lived in poverty |\n\n### Connections to Southern Africa\n\nBritain\'s industrial growth had direct consequences for southern Africa:\n- The demand for **raw materials** (wool, hides, ivory, later diamonds and gold) drove colonial expansion\n- British **capital** (money) would later fund mining operations in South Africa\n- British **technology** (railways, machinery) transformed the southern African economy\n- The social changes of the Industrial Revolution — trade unions, class conflict — would shape South African history\n\nThe Industrial Revolution thus set the stage for British involvement in southern Africa, particularly the **mineral revolution** that would begin with the discovery of diamonds in the 1860s.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Southern Africa Before 1860 and Diamond Mining in Kimberley ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Southern Africa Before 1860\n\nBefore the discovery of diamonds and gold, southern Africa was a largely **agricultural region** with a mix of different peoples and political systems.\n\n### Political Settlement\n\n| Group | Area | Economy |\n|-------|------|----------|\n| **Xhosa chiefdoms** | Eastern Cape frontier | Cattle herding, agriculture |\n| **Zulu Kingdom** | KwaZulu-Natal | Powerful military state under Shaka and successors |\n| **Sotho, Tswana, Pedi** | Interior (highveld) | Mixed farming, cattle herding |\n| **San and Khoikhoi** | Western and northern Cape | Hunting and gathering; pastoralism |\n| **British settlers** | Cape Colony, Natal | Commercial farming, trade |\n| **Boer/Afrikaner republics** | Transvaal (ZAR), Orange Free State | Subsistence farming, cattle ranching |\n\n### The Cape Colony\n\n- Under British control since 1806\n- An agricultural economy based on wine, wheat, wool, and cattle\n- Cape Town was a port town connecting Britain to India and the East\n- A small, relatively quiet colonial settlement compared to what would follow\n\n### The Boer Republics\n\n- After the **Great Trek** (1830s-1840s), Boer (Afrikaner) settlers moved into the interior\n- They established the **South African Republic (Transvaal)** and the **Orange Free State**\n- These were farming communities with limited international trade\n- Conflict with indigenous peoples over land was constant'),
  q(2, 'What was the economy of southern Africa like before the discovery of diamonds?',
    ['It was largely agricultural, based on farming, cattle herding, and limited trade', 'It was a major industrial economy', 'It was based entirely on mining', 'It had no economy at all'], 0,
    'Before the mineral revolution, southern Africa was predominantly agricultural. Both African societies and white settlers depended on farming and cattle. The discovery of diamonds in the late 1860s would completely transform this.'),
  t(3, '## The Discovery of Diamonds\n\nIn **1867**, a 15-year-old boy named **Erasmus Jacobs** found a shiny pebble near the Orange River on a farm called De Kalk, near Hopetown. It turned out to be a **21.25-carat diamond**, later named the "Eureka."\n\nIn **1869**, an even larger diamond — the **83.5-carat "Star of South Africa"** — was found by a Griqua shepherd boy. This discovery sparked a **diamond rush**.\n\n### The Diamond Fields\n\n- Thousands of prospectors rushed to the area around what is now **Kimberley** in the Northern Cape\n- The main mining sites were at **Colesberg Kopje** (later the Big Hole), Dutoitspan, Bultfontein, and De Beers\n- By 1871, an estimated **50 000 people** had flocked to the diamond fields\n- The area was initially in disputed territory claimed by the Griqua people, the Orange Free State, and the Transvaal\n\n### British Annexation\n\n- In 1871, the British government annexed the diamond fields, declaring them the colony of **Griqualand West**\n- The Orange Free State protested but was overruled\n- This was a clear example of how **mineral wealth** drove colonial expansion\n- The Griqua leader **Waterboer** had requested British protection, which Britain used as justification'),
  fb(4, 'The first diamond in South Africa was found in ___ near Hopetown. The diamond fields were annexed by Britain in 1871 and called Griqualand ___.',
    ['1867', 'West'],
    'The discovery of the Eureka diamond in 1867 was followed by the Star of South Africa in 1869, triggering a massive diamond rush. Britain annexed the diamond fields in 1871, creating the colony of Griqualand West — an early example of how mineral wealth shaped colonial borders.'),
  t(5, '## Diamond Mining at Kimberley\n\n### Early Open-Cast Mining\n\n- At first, individual diggers staked **claims** — small plots of ground that they dug by hand\n- Each claim was about 10 m × 10 m\n- Thousands of claims were packed together, creating chaos:\n  - Roads between claims collapsed as digging went deeper\n  - Water flooded the deeper claims\n  - Rockfalls became common and deadly\n  - Transporting soil and rock to the surface became increasingly difficult\n\n### The Move to Deep-Level Mining\n\nAs mining went deeper, individual diggers could no longer afford the equipment needed:\n- **Pumps** to remove water\n- **Hauling equipment** to lift rock from great depths\n- **Dynamite** to blast through hard rock\n- Only **wealthy companies** with capital could afford this technology\n\n### Cecil John Rhodes and De Beers\n\n- **Cecil John Rhodes** arrived at the diamond fields as a young man in 1871\n- He gradually bought up smaller claims and companies\n- In **1888**, he merged with his rival Barney Barnato to form **De Beers Consolidated Mines**\n- De Beers controlled approximately **90% of the world\'s diamond production**\n- This was a **monopoly** — one company controlling an entire industry\n\n### The Big Hole\n\nThe Kimberley Mine (the "Big Hole") was dug entirely by hand between 1871 and 1914. It is approximately **463 metres wide** and **240 metres deep** — one of the largest hand-dug excavations in the world.'),
  q(6, 'Why did diamond mining shift from individual diggers to large companies like De Beers?',
    ['Deep-level mining required expensive equipment (pumps, hauling gear, dynamite) that only wealthy companies could afford', 'Individual diggers preferred to work for companies', 'The government banned individual mining', 'There were no more diamonds near the surface'], 0,
    'As mining went deeper, the costs escalated dramatically. Water flooding, rockfalls, and the need for heavy machinery made it impossible for small-scale individual diggers to continue. Wealthy capitalists like Cecil Rhodes bought up claims and consolidated them into large mining companies.'),
  t(7, '## Regulating Supply and the Price of Diamonds\n\nOnce De Beers controlled most of the world\'s diamond production, Rhodes implemented a strategy to keep diamond prices high.\n\n### How the Monopoly Worked\n\n1. **Control supply**: De Beers limited the number of diamonds released onto the market\n2. **Stockpile**: Excess diamonds were stored in vaults rather than sold\n3. **Fix prices**: By controlling supply, De Beers could set prices artificially high\n4. **One-channel marketing**: All diamonds were sold through a single sales channel\n\n### The "One Man, One Claim" Rule\n\nEarly regulations limited each person to one claim. This rule was used to:\n- Prevent black diggers from accumulating wealth\n- Force black diggers to sell to white claim holders or become labourers\n- Concentrate ownership in the hands of white capitalists\n\n### Impact on Workers\n\n- As companies grew, they needed **cheap labour** in large quantities\n- Black workers were recruited from across southern Africa\n- They were paid very low wages compared to white workers\n- The foundations of South Africa\'s **migrant labour system** were established at Kimberley\n- This system would define South African mining — and society — for over a century'),
  fb(8, 'Cecil John Rhodes formed ___ Consolidated Mines in 1888, creating a monopoly over the diamond industry. De Beers controlled approximately ___% of the world\'s diamond production.',
    ['De Beers', '90'],
    'Rhodes merged with Barney Barnato to create De Beers Consolidated Mines, which controlled nearly all diamond production worldwide. By limiting supply, De Beers kept diamond prices artificially high — a strategy that continued for over a century.'),
  q(9, 'What is a monopoly?',
    ['When one company or person controls an entire industry or market', 'When many small companies compete equally', 'When the government runs all businesses', 'When workers own the means of production'], 0,
    'A monopoly exists when one company has exclusive control over a product or industry. De Beers\' monopoly over diamonds meant it could control supply and set prices without competition. This made Rhodes one of the wealthiest and most powerful men in the British Empire.'),
  t(10, '## Conditions for Black Workers at Kimberley\n\nThe diamond fields created a new pattern of labour exploitation that would shape South Africa\'s future.\n\n### The Compound System\n\n- From the 1880s, black mineworkers were housed in **closed compounds** — fenced, guarded camps next to the mines\n- Workers were locked inside for the duration of their contracts (typically 3-6 months)\n- They could not leave the compound or receive visitors\n- The stated reason was to prevent **diamond theft (IDB — Illicit Diamond Buying)**\n- In reality, compounds gave mine owners total control over workers\n\n### Conditions in the Compounds\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Housing** | Crowded dormitories; no privacy |\n| **Food** | Basic rations provided by the company |\n| **Health** | Disease spread easily; medical care was minimal |\n| **Freedom** | Workers were searched (including strip searches) when leaving |\n| **Wages** | Very low — a fraction of what white workers earned |\n\n### Pass Laws\n\n- Black workers were required to carry **passes** proving they had permission to be in the mining area\n- Workers without passes could be arrested\n- This was an early version of the pass laws that would later be central to apartheid\n\n### Legacy\n\nThe Kimberley compound system and migrant labour pattern became the model for the entire South African mining industry. The **exploitation of cheap black labour** to maximise profits for white-owned companies was a foundation of the South African economy — and of the inequality that persists today.'),
];

