// --- Lesson 1: Maps, Atlases, and the Globe ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Maps and Atlases\n\nA **map** is a flat (two-dimensional) representation of the Earth\'s surface or a part of it. An **atlas** is a collection of maps bound together in a book.\n\n### Kinds of Maps\n\n| Map Type | Purpose | Example |\n|----------|---------|----------|\n| **Political maps** | Show country and province boundaries | Map of South Africa showing 9 provinces |\n| **Physical maps** | Show landforms — mountains, rivers, plains | Map showing the Drakensberg mountains |\n| **Topographic maps** | Show detailed natural and human features using symbols and contour lines | 1:50 000 maps from the Chief Directorate |\n| **Street/road maps** | Show roads and streets for navigation | A map of Johannesburg\'s roads |\n| **Thematic maps** | Show specific data — rainfall, population, resources | South Africa\'s annual rainfall map |\n| **Cadastral maps** | Show property boundaries and ownership | Used by municipalities for land registration |\n\n### Scales on Maps\n\nScale tells you the relationship between distance on a map and distance on the ground.\n\n| Scale Type | Example | Explanation |\n|------------|---------|-------------|\n| **Word scale** | "1 cm represents 5 km" | Written in words |\n| **Ratio/number scale** | 1:50 000 | 1 cm on the map equals 50 000 cm on the ground |\n| **Line scale (bar scale)** | A drawn bar with distances marked | Most reliable — still accurate if the map is resized |'),
  q(2, 'Which type of scale remains accurate even if a map is enlarged or reduced in size?',
    ['Line scale (bar scale)', 'Ratio scale', 'Word scale', 'Number scale'], 0,
    'A line (bar) scale is a physical line drawn on the map with distances marked. When the map is enlarged or reduced, the line scale changes proportionally, so it always shows the correct distances. Ratio and word scales become incorrect if the map size changes.'),
  t(3, '## Calculating Distances Using Scale\n\nOne of the most important map skills is converting between map distance and actual (real-world) distance.\n\n### Using a Ratio Scale\n\n**Formula:** Actual distance = Map distance × Scale factor\n\n*Example with 1:50 000 scale:*\n1. Measure the distance on the map in centimetres (e.g., 4.5 cm)\n2. Multiply by the scale: 4.5 × 50 000 = 225 000 cm\n3. Convert to metres: 225 000 ÷ 100 = 2 250 m\n4. Convert to kilometres: 2 250 ÷ 1 000 = **2.25 km**\n\n### Other Scales to Know\n\n| Scale | 1 cm on the map = |\n|-------|--------------------|\n| 1:10 000 | 100 m |\n| 1:50 000 | 500 m |\n| 1:250 000 | 2.5 km |\n| 1:1 000 000 | 10 km |\n\n### Large-Scale vs Small-Scale Maps\n\n- **Large-scale maps** (e.g., 1:10 000): Show a **small area** in **great detail** — street maps, building plans\n- **Small-scale maps** (e.g., 1:1 000 000): Show a **large area** with **less detail** — country or continent maps\n\nRemember: "Large scale, small area, lots of detail" and "Small scale, large area, less detail."'),
  fb(4, 'On a 1:50 000 map, 1 cm equals ___ metres on the ground. A large-scale map shows a small area in ___ detail.',
    ['500', 'great'],
    'At 1:50 000 scale, 1 cm × 50 000 = 50 000 cm = 500 m. Large-scale maps have a larger ratio (like 1:10 000) meaning each centimetre represents less ground distance, so you see more detail for a smaller area.'),
  t(5, '## The Globe\n\nA **globe** is a three-dimensional (spherical) model of the Earth. It is the most accurate representation of the Earth because it shows the correct shape, size, and position of continents and oceans.\n\n### Advantages and Disadvantages of Globes\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|------------|---------------|\n| Most accurate representation of Earth | Cannot show small areas in detail |\n| Shows correct shapes and sizes | Difficult to carry around |\n| Shows distances accurately | Cannot see the whole Earth at once |\n| Shows the Earth\'s tilt (23.5°) | Expensive to produce |\n\n### Lines on the Globe\n\n| Line | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Equator** | 0° latitude; divides the Earth into Northern and Southern Hemispheres |\n| **Tropic of Cancer** | 23.5°N; the sun is directly overhead here on 21 June |\n| **Tropic of Capricorn** | 23.5°S; the sun is directly overhead here on 21 December |\n| **Arctic Circle** | 66.5°N; marks the start of the polar region |\n| **Antarctic Circle** | 66.5°S; marks the start of the southern polar region |\n| **Prime Meridian** | 0° longitude; runs through Greenwich, London; divides Earth into Eastern and Western Hemispheres |\n| **International Date Line** | 180° longitude; where the date changes |'),
  q(6, 'Which line of latitude divides the Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres?',
    ['The Equator (0° latitude)', 'The Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N)', 'The Prime Meridian (0° longitude)', 'The International Date Line (180°)'], 0,
    'The Equator is the line of 0° latitude that circles the Earth at its widest point, dividing it into the Northern Hemisphere (north of the Equator) and the Southern Hemisphere (south of the Equator).'),
  t(7, '## Latitude and Longitude\n\n### Latitude\n\n- Lines of latitude run **east to west** (horizontally) around the Earth\n- They measure distance **north or south** of the Equator\n- Range: 0° (Equator) to 90°N (North Pole) or 90°S (South Pole)\n- Lines of latitude are also called **parallels** because they are parallel to each other\n- South Africa lies between approximately **22°S and 35°S**\n\n### Longitude\n\n- Lines of longitude run **north to south** (vertically) from pole to pole\n- They measure distance **east or west** of the Prime Meridian (Greenwich)\n- Range: 0° to 180°E or 180°W\n- Lines of longitude are also called **meridians**\n- They are NOT parallel — they converge at the poles\n- South Africa lies between approximately **17°E and 33°E**\n\n### Using Coordinates\n\nEvery place on Earth can be identified by its coordinates (latitude, longitude):\n- **Cape Town**: 33°55\'S, 18°25\'E\n- **Johannesburg**: 26°12\'S, 28°02\'E\n- **Durban**: 29°51\'S, 31°01\'E\n\n**Remember:** Latitude comes first (like the alphabet — "lat" before "long").'),
  fb(8, 'Lines of latitude run east to west and are also called ___. Lines of longitude run north to south and are also called ___.',
    ['parallels', 'meridians'],
    'Lines of latitude are called parallels because they are always the same distance apart. Lines of longitude are called meridians; they converge (meet) at the North and South Poles.'),
  q(9, 'What are the approximate latitude coordinates of South Africa?',
    ['Between 22°S and 35°S', 'Between 22°N and 35°N', 'Between 0° and 10°S', 'Between 40°S and 60°S'], 0,
    'South Africa is located entirely in the Southern Hemisphere, between approximately 22°S (the border with Botswana/Limpopo) and 35°S (Cape Agulhas, the southernmost point of Africa).'),
  t(10, '## Hemispheres and the Earth\'s Rotation\n\nThe Earth\'s position and movement affect our daily lives.\n\n### The Earth\'s Revolution Around the Sun\n\n- The Earth takes **365.25 days** (one year) to orbit the sun\n- The Earth\'s axis is tilted at **23.5°** — this tilt causes the **seasons**\n- When the Southern Hemisphere is tilted towards the sun (December), South Africa experiences **summer**\n- When it is tilted away (June), South Africa experiences **winter**\n\n### The Earth\'s Rotation\n\n- The Earth rotates on its axis once every **24 hours** — this causes **day and night**\n- It rotates from **west to east**, which is why the sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west\n\n### Time Zones\n\n- The Earth is divided into **24 time zones** (360° ÷ 15° = 24 zones)\n- Each zone represents one hour of time difference\n- **South African Standard Time (SAST)** is **UTC+2** (2 hours ahead of Greenwich Mean Time)\n- The **International Date Line** (180°) is where the date changes: when you cross it going west, you move forward one day; going east, you go back one day\n\n### Practical Application\n\nIf it is 12:00 noon in South Africa (UTC+2), what time is it in:\n- **London** (UTC+0): 10:00 (2 hours behind)\n- **New York** (UTC-5): 05:00 (7 hours behind)\n- **Sydney** (UTC+10): 20:00 (8 hours ahead)'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Satellite Images ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Are Satellite Images?\n\nA **satellite image** is a photograph of the Earth taken from a **satellite** orbiting in space. Satellites use special sensors to capture images of the Earth\'s surface.\n\n### How Satellite Images Are Taken\n\n1. Satellites orbit the Earth at different altitudes (heights)\n2. Sensors on the satellite detect **electromagnetic radiation** (visible light, infrared, and other wavelengths) reflected or emitted by the Earth\'s surface\n3. The data is sent to **ground stations** on Earth\n4. Computers process the data into images we can see and interpret\n\n### Types of Satellites\n\n| Type | Altitude | Coverage | Use |\n|------|----------|----------|------|\n| **Geostationary** | 36 000 km | Same area continuously | Weather monitoring |\n| **Polar-orbiting** | 200-1 000 km | Different strips each orbit | Detailed surface mapping |\n\n### Satellite Images vs Aerial Photographs\n\n| Feature | Satellite Image | Aerial Photograph |\n|---------|-----------------|--------------------|\n| **Taken from** | Space (satellite) | Aircraft |\n| **Scale** | Smaller (covers large area) | Larger (covers smaller area) |\n| **Sensors** | Multiple wavelengths (including invisible) | Visible light |\n| **Frequency** | Can take images repeatedly | Once per flight |\n| **Cost** | Expensive to launch, but images shared freely | Expensive per flight |'),
  q(2, 'What is the main difference between satellite images and aerial photographs?',
    ['Satellite images are taken from space and can use invisible wavelengths, while aerial photographs are taken from aircraft using visible light', 'Satellite images are always black and white', 'Aerial photographs show larger areas', 'There is no difference between them'], 0,
    'Satellite sensors can detect electromagnetic radiation beyond what the human eye can see — including infrared and ultraviolet. This allows satellite images to show things like vegetation health, temperature, and moisture that are invisible in ordinary photographs.'),
  t(3, '## Reading Satellite Images\n\nSatellite images use colours to represent different features on the Earth\'s surface.\n\n### True-Colour vs False-Colour Images\n\n| Type | Description | Use |\n|------|-------------|------|\n| **True-colour** | Shows features as they appear to the human eye | General viewing — similar to a photograph |\n| **False-colour** | Uses artificially assigned colours to highlight specific features | Scientific analysis — e.g., healthy vegetation appears bright red |\n\n### Identifying Features on Satellite Images\n\n| Feature | Appearance |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Water** | Dark blue or black (deep water); lighter blue (shallow) |\n| **Vegetation** | Green (true-colour); bright red (false-colour infrared) |\n| **Bare soil/desert** | Brown, tan, or yellow |\n| **Urban areas** | Grey or white; regular geometric patterns |\n| **Clouds** | Bright white, often with shadows |\n| **Snow/ice** | Bright white (similar to clouds but at ground level) |\n| **Smoke/haze** | Grey-white wisps |\n\n### Seasonal Changes\n\nSatellite images taken at different times of year show seasonal changes:\n- **Summer**: More vegetation (greener), longer day lengths\n- **Winter**: Less vegetation (browner), shorter days\n- **Drought**: Brown, dry landscape compared to wetter years'),
  fb(4, 'In a false-colour satellite image, healthy vegetation typically appears bright ___. Deep water appears ___ or black on satellite images.',
    ['red', 'dark blue'],
    'False-colour images use infrared wavelengths to highlight vegetation — healthy plants reflect a lot of infrared light, which is displayed as bright red. Water absorbs most wavelengths, so deep water appears very dark blue or black.'),
  t(5, '## Uses of Satellite Images\n\nSatellite images have many practical applications, especially in South Africa:\n\n### Weather Forecasting\n\n- Weather satellites track **cloud patterns**, storms, and cold fronts\n- The South African Weather Service uses satellite data daily\n- **Meteosat** satellites (geostationary) provide continuous images of weather over Africa\n- Farmers, disaster managers, and the public all rely on satellite-based weather forecasts\n\n### Environmental Monitoring\n\n| Application | Example |\n|-------------|----------|\n| **Deforestation** | Tracking loss of forests in the Amazon or indigenous forests in South Africa |\n| **Veld fires** | Detecting and monitoring bush fires in real time |\n| **Flooding** | Mapping flood extent for disaster response |\n| **Erosion** | Monitoring soil erosion and land degradation |\n| **Pollution** | Tracking air and water pollution |\n| **Climate change** | Measuring ice sheet melting, sea level rise, temperature changes |\n\n### Agriculture\n\n- Farmers use satellite data to monitor **crop health** and **soil moisture**\n- **Precision farming**: Applying water and fertiliser only where needed, based on satellite data\n- Estimating **crop yields** before harvest — important for food security planning\n- Identifying areas affected by **drought** or **pest infestation**'),
  q(6, 'How do satellite images help farmers in South Africa?',
    ['They monitor crop health, soil moisture, and drought conditions to help farmers make better decisions', 'They replace the need for farming altogether', 'They only help farmers in other countries', 'They are too expensive for any practical farming use'], 0,
    'Satellite images provide farmers with valuable information about their crops and land conditions. They can identify stressed crops (using infrared data), monitor soil moisture, track drought conditions, and plan irrigation more efficiently.'),
  t(7, '## Information From Satellite Images: Water, Vegetation, Land Use, and Clouds\n\nSatellite images provide specific kinds of information that are useful for planning and decision-making.\n\n### Water\n\n- Satellites can monitor **dam levels**, river flows, and groundwater\n- In South Africa, this is critical for managing scarce water resources\n- Satellite data helps identify areas at risk of **drought or flooding**\n\n### Vegetation Patterns\n\n- The **Normalised Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI)** measures vegetation health from satellite data\n- Healthy vegetation reflects more infrared light\n- NDVI maps show where vegetation is thriving (green) or stressed (brown/yellow)\n- This helps monitor **deforestation**, **desertification**, and **land degradation**\n\n### Land Use\n\n- Satellites track how land is being used: farming, urban development, mining, natural vegetation\n- Over time, satellite images show **land use change** — e.g., urban sprawl replacing farmland\n- In South Africa, this is important for planning and environmental management\n\n### Cloud Patterns\n\n- Cloud patterns reveal weather systems: cold fronts, warm fronts, tropical cyclones\n- The movement of clouds helps predict rainfall and storms\n- In southern Africa, satellite-tracked weather systems help predict seasonal rainfall patterns'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ measures vegetation health from satellite data by comparing how much infrared light plants reflect. In South Africa, satellite monitoring of ___ levels helps manage scarce water resources.',
    ['NDVI', 'dam'],
    'The Normalised Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) uses the difference between visible and infrared light to assess plant health. South Africa monitors dam levels using satellite imagery, which is essential for water resource management in this water-scarce country.'),
  q(9, 'Why are satellite images particularly useful for monitoring land use change over time?',
    ['Satellites can take repeated images of the same area, allowing comparison over months or years', 'Satellite images can only be taken once', 'They are too blurry to show changes', 'Land use never changes in South Africa'], 0,
    'Satellites orbit the Earth repeatedly, allowing images of the same location to be captured over days, months, or years. By comparing these images, scientists can track changes like urban expansion, deforestation, or dam levels.'),
  t(10, '## Satellite Technology in the South African Context\n\nSouth Africa is an active participant in satellite technology and remote sensing.\n\n### South African Satellite Programmes\n\n- **SumbandilaSat**: South Africa\'s first earth observation microsatellite, launched in 2009\n- **SANSA** (South African National Space Agency): Manages South Africa\'s space science and technology programmes\n- SANSA operates a satellite receiving station at **Hartebeesthoek** near Pretoria\n- South Africa is part of the **Square Kilometre Array (SKA)** radio telescope project in the Northern Cape\n\n### Practical Applications in South Africa\n\n| Application | How It Helps |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Disaster management** | Flood mapping, fire detection, drought monitoring |\n| **Urban planning** | Tracking urban growth and informal settlement expansion |\n| **Agriculture** | Crop monitoring, yield estimation, irrigation planning |\n| **Mining** | Environmental monitoring around mines |\n| **Conservation** | Monitoring national parks and marine protected areas |\n| **Water management** | Tracking dam levels and river systems |\n\n### Google Earth\n\nWhile not a real-time satellite, **Google Earth** uses satellite and aerial imagery to create a digital model of the Earth. It is freely available and widely used in South African schools for:\n- Virtual field trips to any location on Earth\n- Measuring distances and areas\n- Exploring different landscapes and land uses\n- Comparing historical and current images of an area'),
];

