// CHAPTER 3 (History – Term 2): The Mineral Revolution in South Africa
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Britain, Diamond Mining, and Increasing Labour Control ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Mineral Revolution: A Turning Point\n\nThe discovery of diamonds (1867) near Kimberley and gold (1886) on the Witwatersrand fundamentally changed southern Africa. Historians call this transformation the **mineral revolution**.\n\n### What Changed?\n\n| Before Mineral Revolution | After Mineral Revolution |\n|--------------------------|-------------------------|\n| Agricultural economy | Mining-based economy |\n| Small, scattered settlements | Large cities (Johannesburg, Kimberley) |\n| Limited infrastructure | Railways, roads, telegraph lines |\n| Relatively self-sufficient African communities | Migrant labour system |\n| Limited British interest in the interior | Intense British imperial interest |\n| Small-scale trade | Integration into the global capitalist economy |\n\n### Britain\'s Industrial Revolution Connection\n\n- Britain\'s factories needed **raw materials** — diamonds and gold were incredibly valuable\n- British **capital** (money) flowed into South African mining\n- British **technology** (steam engines, railways, dynamite) made deep-level mining possible\n- British **investors** demanded political control to protect their investments\n- The mineral revolution tied South Africa\'s economy to the British Empire'),
  q(2, 'Why is the discovery of diamonds and gold called a "revolution"?',
    ['Because it fundamentally transformed South Africa from an agricultural society to a mining-based industrial economy', 'Because there was a violent overthrow of government', 'Because it had no lasting effects', 'Because only a few people were affected'], 0,
    'The mineral revolution was a fundamental transformation comparable to the Industrial Revolution in Britain. It changed the economy, society, politics, and race relations in southern Africa, creating patterns of inequality that persist today.'),
  t(3, '## Increasing Labour Control Over Black Workers\n\nAs mining companies grew more powerful, they developed increasingly harsh systems to control black workers and keep wages low.\n\n### The Pass System\n\n- All black workers in the mining areas were required to carry a **pass** at all times\n- The pass showed who the worker\'s employer was and whether they had permission to be in the area\n- Workers without valid passes could be **arrested**\n- The pass system restricted the **free movement** of black people\n- It allowed employers and the state to control where black workers lived and worked\n\n### The Compound System\n\n- Black mineworkers were housed in **closed compounds** — large, fenced camps next to the mines\n- Workers could not leave the compound during their contract period\n- Compounds were designed to:\n  - **Prevent diamond theft** (the official reason)\n  - **Control workers** (the real reason)\n  - **Keep wages low** (workers had few expenses, so employers argued they needed less pay)\n  - **Prevent workers from organising** or forming unions\n\n### Contract Labour\n\n- Black workers signed **contracts** for fixed periods (3-6 months)\n- After the contract ended, they had to return to their rural homes\n- This **migrant labour system** meant families were separated for months at a time\n- It prevented black workers from settling permanently in mining towns\n- Workers had no job security and could not negotiate wages'),
  fb(4, 'Black workers were housed in closed ___ near the mines where they could not leave during their contracts. The ___ system required all black workers to carry documents proving their right to be in mining areas.',
    ['compounds', 'pass'],
    'The compound system and pass laws gave mine owners and the state almost total control over black workers. These systems were not just about preventing diamond theft — they were tools of labour control that kept wages low and workers powerless.'),
  t(5, '## Further Land Dispossession and the Defeat of African Kingdoms\n\nThe mineral revolution accelerated the loss of African land and independence.\n\n### Why Was Land Taken?\n\n1. **Mining land**: Mineral-rich land was seized for mining operations\n2. **Labour supply**: Dispossessing Africans of their land forced them to work in the mines for wages\n3. **Settler farming**: White farmers took fertile land for commercial agriculture\n4. **Strategic control**: Controlling territory gave Britain power over routes and resources\n\n### The Defeat of African Kingdoms\n\n| Kingdom/People | Date | What Happened |\n|----------------|------|---------------|\n| **Xhosa** | 1878 | Final frontier wars; loss of remaining independence |\n| **Pedi (Northern Sotho)** | 1879 | Defeated by a combined British-Boer force |\n| **Zulu** | 1879 | Anglo-Zulu War; Zulu Kingdom destroyed despite initial victory at Isandlwana |\n| **Griqua** | 1871-1880s | Lost land to diamond mining and British annexation |\n\n### The Anglo-Zulu War (1879)\n\n- Britain invaded the Zulu Kingdom under King **Cetshwayo** in January 1879\n- At the **Battle of Isandlwana** (22 January 1879), the Zulus won a stunning victory, destroying a British force of 1 300 soldiers\n- However, Britain eventually defeated the Zulus at **Ulundi** (4 July 1879) using superior firepower\n- The Zulu Kingdom was broken up and Cetshwayo was exiled\n- The destruction of African kingdoms removed obstacles to colonial expansion and ensured a supply of migrant labour for the mines'),
  q(6, 'How did land dispossession help the mining industry?',
    ['By forcing Africans off their land, it made them dependent on wage labour in the mines', 'It had no connection to mining', 'It reduced the number of available workers', 'It gave land to African communities'], 0,
    'When African communities lost their land, they could no longer sustain themselves through farming. They were forced to seek wage employment in the mines and on white-owned farms. This deliberate process created the cheap labour supply that the mining industry needed.'),
  t(7, '## The Impact of Migrant Labour on Families\n\nThe migrant labour system had devastating social consequences that are still felt today.\n\n### How the System Worked\n\n1. Men left their rural homes to work in the mines for months at a time\n2. They lived in compounds near the mines\n3. They sent part of their wages home to support their families\n4. When the contract ended, they returned home briefly before signing a new contract\n5. Women, children, and the elderly remained in the rural areas\n\n### Effects on Families\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Family separation** | Husbands away from wives and children for most of the year |\n| **Women\'s burden** | Women had to do all farming, child-rearing, and household tasks alone |\n| **Children** | Grew up without fathers; emotional and social impacts |\n| **Health** | Workers contracted diseases (tuberculosis, silicosis) in the mines and brought them home |\n| **Rural poverty** | Not enough labour to farm productively; dependence on mine wages |\n| **Social breakdown** | Alcoholism, infidelity, and family conflict increased |\n\n### Legacy\n\nThe migrant labour system created patterns of family disruption and rural poverty that persist in South Africa today. Many rural areas remain impoverished, with working-age adults still migrating to cities and mines for employment.'),
  fb(8, 'Under the migrant labour system, men left their ___ homes to work in the mines while women, children, and the elderly stayed behind. Workers often contracted ___ and silicosis in the mines.',
    ['rural', 'tuberculosis'],
    'The migrant labour system tore families apart for generations. Tuberculosis (TB) and silicosis (caused by breathing in rock dust) were — and remain — major health problems for South African mineworkers. Many brought these diseases home to their families.'),
  q(9, 'Why did mine owners prefer the migrant labour system?',
    ['It kept wages low because workers\' families remained in rural areas, and it prevented workers from organising permanently', 'It was better for workers\' families', 'The government required it for health reasons', 'Workers preferred to travel long distances'], 0,
    'The migrant labour system benefited mine owners in several ways: wages could be kept low because workers\' families were supported partly by subsistence farming in rural areas; compound living prevented organisation and unions; and workers could be easily replaced when contracts ended.'),
  t(10, '## Indentured Indian Labour in Natal\n\nThe mineral revolution was not the only source of exploited labour in southern Africa.\n\n### Indian Workers in Natal\n\n- From **1860 onwards**, the British colonial government in Natal imported **indentured labourers** from India to work on sugar plantations\n- These workers signed contracts (indentures) committing them to work for 5 years\n- Conditions were harsh: long hours, low pay, physical punishment\n- After their contracts ended, many stayed in South Africa\n- By 1911, there were over **150 000 Indians** in Natal\n\n### Why Indian Labour Was Imported\n\n- The Zulu people refused to work on sugar plantations for the wages offered\n- Plantation owners needed cheap, controllable labour\n- India, under British colonial rule, was a source of workers who could be recruited under contract\n\n### Passenger Indians\n\n- A second group of Indians came to South Africa as **free immigrants** ("passenger Indians")\n- They were mostly traders and merchants from Gujarat\n- They established businesses in Natal and the Transvaal\n- **Mohandas Gandhi** arrived in South Africa in 1893 and spent 21 years fighting discrimination against Indians\n- Gandhi developed the strategy of **satyagraha** (passive resistance) in South Africa before taking it to India\n\nThe importation of Indian workers added another dimension to South Africa\'s complex racial and social landscape.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Deep-Level Gold Mining on the Witwatersrand ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Discovery of Gold\n\nIn **1886**, an Australian prospector named **George Harrison** discovered gold on the farm Langlaagte on the **Witwatersrand** (a ridge of hills in what is now Gauteng Province). This discovery would transform South Africa forever.\n\n### Why Witwatersrand Gold Was Different\n\n| Feature | Alluvial Gold (e.g., California) | Witwatersrand Gold |\n|---------|--------------------------------|--------------------|\n| **Location** | In river beds and streams | Locked in hard quartzite rock deep underground |\n| **Mining method** | Panning and simple digging | Deep-level mining with heavy machinery |\n| **Capital needed** | Small — individual prospectors could mine | Huge — only wealthy companies could afford it |\n| **Labour needed** | Individual or small groups | Thousands of workers per mine |\n| **Duration** | Often exhausted quickly | Enormous deposits — mining continues today |\n\nThe Witwatersrand contained the **largest gold deposits** ever discovered, but extracting the gold required massive investment and huge numbers of workers.\n\n### The Birth of Johannesburg\n\n- When gold was discovered, the area was part of the **South African Republic (Transvaal)** under President Paul Kruger\n- Within months, a mining camp grew into a town — **Johannesburg**\n- By 1896, Johannesburg had approximately **100 000 residents** — making it the largest city in southern Africa\n- It was rough, dangerous, and multi-cultural — attracting fortune-seekers from around the world'),
  q(2, 'Why did gold mining on the Witwatersrand require large companies rather than individual prospectors?',
    ['The gold was locked in deep underground rock, requiring expensive heavy machinery, dynamite, and thousands of workers', 'Individual prospectors were banned by law', 'The gold was easy to find on the surface', 'Companies were cheaper to operate than individual mines'], 0,
    'Unlike alluvial gold (found in river beds), Witwatersrand gold was trapped in narrow seams of hard quartzite rock deep underground. Extracting it required shafts, tunnels, dynamite, stamp mills, and chemical processing — all of which cost enormous amounts of money.'),
  t(3, '## The Randlords and the Chamber of Mines\n\nThe men who controlled the gold mining industry became known as the **Randlords** — some of the wealthiest and most powerful people in the world.\n\n### Key Randlords\n\n| Name | Background | Significance |\n|------|-----------|---------------|\n| **Cecil John Rhodes** | English; already wealthy from diamonds | Controlled gold mines; became Prime Minister of the Cape Colony |\n| **Barney Barnato** | English; began as a diamond dealer | Rival then partner of Rhodes; major gold mine owner |\n| **Alfred Beit** | German; financier | Key financial backer of gold mining |\n| **J.B. Robinson** | South African; controversial speculator | One of the first to invest in Witwatersrand gold |\n| **Sammy Marks** | Lithuanian-born; industrialist | Mining, farming, and industry in the Transvaal |\n\n### The Chamber of Mines (1889)\n\n- The mining companies formed the **Chamber of Mines** to coordinate their interests\n- The Chamber worked to:\n  - Keep **wages low** through cooperation rather than competition for workers\n  - Recruit labour from across southern Africa (and even further afield)\n  - Lobby the government for favourable policies\n  - Standardise mining practices\n- The Chamber became one of the most powerful organisations in South Africa'),
  fb(4, 'The wealthy men who controlled the gold mining industry were called ___. The ___ of Mines was formed in 1889 to coordinate the mining companies\' interests.',
    ['Randlords', 'Chamber'],
    'The Randlords amassed enormous fortunes from gold and diamonds. The Chamber of Mines allowed mining companies to work together to keep wages low and influence government policy, rather than competing with each other for workers.'),
  t(5, '## Working Conditions on the Gold Mines\n\nGold mining on the Witwatersrand was extremely dangerous and exploitative.\n\n### The Mining Process\n\n1. **Shafts** were sunk deep into the ground (some over 3 km deep today)\n2. Horizontal **tunnels** (stopes) were blasted out of the rock using **dynamite**\n3. Workers drilled holes into the rock face for explosives\n4. After blasting, workers removed the broken rock and sent it to the surface\n5. On the surface, rock was crushed in **stamp mills** and gold was extracted using chemicals\n\n### Skilled and Unskilled Labour\n\n| Category | Workers | Wages | Conditions |\n|----------|---------|-------|------------|\n| **Skilled** | White workers (mainly British and Afrikaner) | High wages | Supervised operations; operated machinery |\n| **Unskilled** | Black workers from across southern Africa | Very low wages (about 1/10 of white wages) | Dangerous manual labour underground |\n\n### The Colour Bar\n\nA **colour bar** prevented black workers from doing skilled jobs, regardless of their ability:\n- Only white workers could be licensed as **blasters** (using dynamite), engine drivers, or supervisors\n- Black workers were restricted to manual labour: drilling, shovelling, loading\n- This ensured white workers received higher wages and kept black workers in subordinate positions\n- The colour bar was both **custom** (informal practice) and later **law** (the Mines and Works Act of 1911)'),
  q(6, 'What was the "colour bar" in South African mining?',
    ['A system that reserved skilled, high-paying jobs for white workers and restricted black workers to manual labour', 'A coloured bar where miners could drink', 'A law that banned mining by all races', 'An equal opportunity employment system'], 0,
    'The colour bar was a system of racial discrimination in the workplace. It prevented black workers from doing skilled jobs (like blasting, machine operation, or supervision), regardless of their ability. This kept white wages high and black wages extremely low.'),
  t(7, '## Forms of Labour Resistance\n\nDespite harsh conditions, black and white workers resisted exploitation in different ways.\n\n### Black Worker Resistance\n\n| Form | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Desertion** | Workers abandoned their contracts and returned home — the most common form of resistance |\n| **Go-slow** | Workers deliberately worked slowly to protest conditions |\n| **Sabotage** | Workers damaged equipment or wasted materials |\n| **Refusal to re-contract** | Workers chose not to sign new contracts after their term ended |\n| **Strikes** | Workers stopped working collectively — though this was illegal and severely punished |\n\n### White Worker Strikes\n\n- White workers also went on strike, particularly in 1907, 1913, and 1914\n- They protested against mine owners using cheaper black labour to replace them\n- White workers fought to protect the colour bar and their privileged position\n- These strikes were sometimes violent and were put down by the military\n\n### A Key Difference\n\n- White workers fought to **maintain racial privilege** and high wages\n- Black workers fought against **exploitation** and for basic dignity\n- White and black workers rarely made common cause — racial divisions prevented class solidarity'),
  fb(8, 'The most common form of resistance by black mineworkers was ___ — leaving their contracts and returning home. White workers fought to protect the colour ___ that reserved skilled jobs for them.',
    ['desertion', 'bar'],
    'Desertion was a form of everyday resistance — workers voted with their feet when conditions were intolerable. White workers\' strikes were aimed at maintaining their racial privileges, not at creating equality. This racial division among workers served the interests of mine owners.'),
  q(9, 'Why did white and black mineworkers rarely unite against the mine owners?',
    ['Racial divisions and the colour bar meant white workers fought to keep their privileged position rather than ally with black workers', 'They worked in different countries', 'Mine owners treated all workers equally', 'White and black workers spoke the same language and got along well'], 0,
    'The racial hierarchy in mining gave white workers a material interest in maintaining the colour bar. They feared that if black workers could do skilled jobs, mine owners would replace them with cheaper black labour. This racial division weakened the overall labour movement and benefited the mine owners.'),
  t(10, '## The Mineral Revolution as a Turning Point\n\nThe mineral revolution fundamentally changed the balance of power in South Africa.\n\n### The Shifting Balance of Power\n\n- Before 1867, power in southern Africa was divided among African kingdoms, Boer republics, and the British colonies\n- After the mineral revolution, **British imperialism** dominated — backed by mining wealth\n- Cecil Rhodes became **Prime Minister of the Cape Colony** (1890-1896) and used his power to advance British interests\n- The discovery of gold on the Witwatersrand put the **Transvaal** at the centre of British imperial ambitions\n\n### The Road to War\n\n- Tensions between the British and the Boer republics (especially the Transvaal) grew\n- The **Jameson Raid** (1895-1896) — an attempted British-backed overthrow of the Transvaal government — failed but increased hostility\n- The **South African War (Anglo-Boer War, 1899-1902)** was fought largely over control of the goldfields\n- Britain won the war but at enormous cost\n\n### Foundations of the Future\n\nThe mineral revolution established patterns that would define South Africa for the next century:\n- **Migrant labour**: Workers separated from families\n- **Racial discrimination in the workplace**: The colour bar\n- **Pass laws**: Controlling black movement\n- **Compound system**: Controlling black workers\n- **Land dispossession**: Forcing Africans into wage labour\n- These systems were formalised and intensified under **apartheid** after 1948\n\nThe mineral revolution was thus not just an economic event — it was the foundation of modern South Africa\'s deepest problems.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 4 (Geography – Term 2): Climate Regions
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Factors That Influence Temperature and Rainfall in South Africa ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Climate?\n\nBefore studying climate, we must understand the difference between **weather** and **climate**.\n\n### Weather vs Climate\n\n| Feature | Weather | Climate |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| **Definition** | The condition of the atmosphere at a specific place and time | The average weather conditions of a place over a long period (usually 30+ years) |\n| **Timeframe** | Short-term (hours, days) | Long-term (decades) |\n| **Variability** | Changes constantly | Relatively stable patterns |\n| **Example** | "It is raining in Johannesburg today" | "Johannesburg receives about 700 mm of rain per year, mostly in summer" |\n\n### Elements of Weather and Climate\n\n| Element | What It Measures |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Temperature** | How hot or cold the air is (°C) |\n| **Precipitation** | Rain, snow, hail — measured in millimetres (mm) |\n| **Humidity** | The amount of moisture in the air |\n| **Wind** | Direction and speed of air movement |\n| **Air pressure** | The weight of the atmosphere pressing down |\n| **Cloud cover** | How much of the sky is covered by clouds |\n\n### Why Does South Africa\'s Climate Vary?\n\nSouth Africa has a wide range of climates — from desert in the northwest to subtropical on the east coast. This is because of several factors that influence temperature and rainfall.'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between weather and climate?',
    ['Weather is the short-term atmospheric condition at a specific time, while climate is the average weather pattern over 30+ years', 'Weather and climate mean the same thing', 'Climate changes daily while weather stays the same', 'Weather is only about temperature'], 0,
    'Weather is what you experience right now — is it sunny, rainy, hot, cold? Climate is the pattern of weather over decades. You pack your suitcase based on climate (Durban is generally warm) but you may carry an umbrella based on the weather forecast.'),
  t(3, '## Factor 1: Distance From the Equator (Latitude)\n\nLatitude is one of the most important factors influencing temperature.\n\n### How Latitude Affects Temperature\n\n- The **Equator** receives the most direct sunlight because the sun is almost directly overhead throughout the year\n- As you move away from the Equator towards the poles, the sun\'s rays hit the Earth at a **lower angle** (more oblique)\n- Oblique rays spread over a larger area and pass through more atmosphere, so they deliver **less heat**\n- Therefore: **the further from the Equator, the cooler the climate**\n\n### South African Examples\n\n| City | Latitude | Average Annual Temp |\n|------|----------|--------------------|\n| **Musina (Limpopo)** | 22°S | 23°C (closer to Equator, warmer) |\n| **George (Western Cape)** | 34°S | 17°C (further from Equator, cooler) |\n| **Mbombela (Mpumalanga)** | 25°S | 21°C |\n| **Cape Town** | 34°S | 17°C |\n\nSouth Africa lies between approximately 22°S and 35°S, placing it in the **warm temperate** to **subtropical** zone.'),
  fb(4, 'The further a place is from the ___, the cooler its climate tends to be. South Africa lies between approximately 22°S and ___°S.',
    ['Equator', '35'],
    'Latitude is the single most important factor determining temperature. Places near the Equator are warmest because they receive the most direct sunlight. South Africa\'s range of latitudes (22°S to 35°S) contributes to its variety of climates.'),
  t(5, '## Factor 2: Distance From the Sea and Ocean Currents\n\n### The Maritime Effect\n\nThe sea has a **moderating effect** on temperature:\n- Water heats up and cools down more slowly than land\n- Coastal areas have **smaller temperature ranges** (warm winters, cool summers)\n- Inland areas have **larger temperature ranges** (hot summers, cold winters)\n\n### South African Examples\n\n| City | Location | January (summer) | July (winter) | Range |\n|------|----------|-------------------|----------------|--------|\n| **Durban** | Coast | 28°C | 22°C | 6°C |\n| **Johannesburg** | Inland (1 753 m) | 26°C | 16°C | 10°C |\n\n### Ocean Currents\n\nTwo major ocean currents affect South Africa\'s climate:\n\n| Current | Temperature | Location | Effect |\n|---------|------------|----------|--------|\n| **Warm Agulhas Current** | Warm (22-26°C) | East coast (Indian Ocean) | Warms the east coast; brings moisture → more rainfall |\n| **Cold Benguela Current** | Cold (8-14°C) | West coast (Atlantic Ocean) | Cools the west coast; reduces moisture → less rainfall |\n\nThis is why **Durban** (east coast, warm current) is warm and wet, while **Port Nolloth** (west coast, cold current) is cool and dry.'),
  q(6, 'Why does Durban receive much more rainfall than Port Nolloth?',
    ['The warm Agulhas Current on the east coast brings moisture, while the cold Benguela Current on the west coast reduces moisture', 'Durban is at a higher altitude', 'Port Nolloth is closer to the Equator', 'The wind never blows in Port Nolloth'], 0,
    'The warm Agulhas Current heats the air above it, causing evaporation and moisture-laden air that brings rain to the east coast. The cold Benguela Current cools the air, reducing evaporation and rainfall on the west coast. This is why the west coast is semi-desert.'),
  t(7, '## Factor 3: Height Above Sea Level (Altitude)\n\nAltitude has a significant effect on temperature.\n\n### How Altitude Affects Temperature\n\n- Temperature decreases by approximately **6.5°C for every 1 000 m** increase in altitude (the lapse rate)\n- This is why mountains are cooler than lowland areas at the same latitude\n- The interior plateau of South Africa (the highveld) is cooler than the coastal lowlands\n\n### South African Examples\n\n| City | Altitude | Latitude | Average Temp |\n|------|----------|----------|--------------|\n| **Durban** | 8 m | 30°S | 21°C |\n| **Johannesburg** | 1 753 m | 26°S | 16°C |\n| **Bloemfontein** | 1 395 m | 29°S | 16°C |\n\nJohannesburg is **further north** (closer to the Equator) than Durban, yet it is **cooler**. This is because Johannesburg is at a much higher altitude — over 1 700 m above sea level.\n\n## Factor 4: Mountains (Relief)\n\nMountains influence rainfall through **orographic rainfall**:\n\n1. Moist air from the ocean is forced to **rise** over a mountain\n2. As it rises, the air cools and moisture condenses, forming clouds\n3. Rain falls on the **windward side** (facing the wind) of the mountain\n4. The air descends on the other side (the **leeward side** or **rain shadow**), warming and drying out\n5. The leeward side receives much less rain\n\nIn South Africa, the **Drakensberg escarpment** creates a rain shadow effect — the eastern slopes receive heavy rainfall while the interior highveld receives less.'),
  fb(8, 'Temperature decreases by approximately 6.5°C for every 1 000 m increase in ___. The dry area on the sheltered side of a mountain is called the rain ___.',
    ['altitude', 'shadow'],
    'The lapse rate of 6.5°C per 1 000 m explains why high-altitude cities like Johannesburg are cooler despite being closer to the Equator. The rain shadow effect explains why the interior of South Africa is drier than the east coast — the Drakensberg mountains intercept moisture from the Indian Ocean.'),
  q(9, 'Why is Johannesburg cooler than Durban despite being closer to the Equator?',
    ['Because Johannesburg is at a much higher altitude (1 753 m) and temperature decreases with altitude', 'Because Johannesburg is further from the Equator', 'Because Johannesburg is on the coast', 'Because Johannesburg receives more rainfall'], 0,
    'Johannesburg is at 1 753 m above sea level, while Durban is at only 8 m. Using the lapse rate (6.5°C per 1 000 m), the altitude difference accounts for approximately 11°C of cooling. This more than compensates for Johannesburg being slightly closer to the Equator.'),
  t(10, '## South Africa\'s Climate Summary\n\nSouth Africa\'s climate varies enormously due to the combined effects of latitude, altitude, ocean currents, and relief.\n\n### Rainfall Patterns\n\n| Region | Rainfall | Season | Reason |\n|--------|----------|--------|--------|\n| **East coast** (KZN, Eastern Cape) | High (800-1 200 mm/yr) | Summer (Oct-Mar) | Warm Agulhas Current; onshore winds |\n| **Western Cape** | Moderate (500-600 mm/yr) | Winter (Apr-Sep) | Cold fronts from Atlantic |\n| **Interior highveld** (Gauteng, Free State) | Moderate (500-700 mm/yr) | Summer | Convectional rainfall |\n| **West coast** | Very low (50-200 mm/yr) | Sparse | Cold Benguela Current reduces moisture |\n| **Karoo** | Low (200-400 mm/yr) | Erratic | Rain shadow; semi-arid |\n\n### Temperature Patterns\n\n- **Hottest**: Lowveld (Mpumalanga, Limpopo) and Northern Cape — temperatures can exceed 40°C\n- **Coldest**: Drakensberg highlands and interior highveld in winter — frost and occasional snow\n- **Most moderate**: Coastal cities (Cape Town, Durban) — moderated by the ocean\n\n### South African Standard Time\n\n- South Africa uses a single time zone: **SAST (UTC+2)**\n- Based on the 30°E longitude line\n- This means the sun rises and sets at slightly different times in the east and west of the country'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Climate Regions Around the World ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Climate Regions of the World\n\nClimatologists divide the world into major **climate regions** based on temperature and precipitation patterns.\n\n### The Main Climate Zones\n\n| Zone | Location | Temperature | Rainfall |\n|------|----------|------------|----------|\n| **Tropical** | 0°-23.5° (near Equator) | Hot all year (>18°C) | High rainfall (>1 500 mm) |\n| **Subtropical** | 23.5°-35° | Warm to hot | Moderate to high; often seasonal |\n| **Temperate** | 35°-55° | Moderate; distinct seasons | Moderate (500-1 000 mm) |\n| **Continental** | Interior of large landmasses | Extreme range — hot summers, very cold winters | Low to moderate |\n| **Polar** | >66.5° (near poles) | Very cold (<0°C most of year) | Very low (snow/ice) |\n\n### Detailed Climate Types\n\n| Climate Type | Key Features | Example Locations |\n|--------------|-------------|--------------------|\n| **Tropical rainforest** | Hot and wet all year | Congo Basin, Amazon |\n| **Tropical savanna** | Hot; wet and dry seasons | Northern KZN, East Africa |\n| **Desert** | Very hot (or cold); <250 mm rain | Sahara, Namib, Kalahari |\n| **Semi-desert** | Hot; 250-500 mm rain | Northern Cape, Karoo |\n| **Mediterranean** | Warm dry summers; cool wet winters | Western Cape, southern Europe |\n| **Temperate** | Moderate temperatures; rain all year | Eastern Cape, Western Europe |\n| **Tundra** | Very cold; short cool summers | Northern Russia, Canada |\n| **High mountain** | Cold; varies with altitude | Drakensberg, Himalayas |'),
  q(2, 'Which climate type is characterised by warm dry summers and cool wet winters?',
    ['Mediterranean climate', 'Tropical rainforest', 'Desert climate', 'Tundra'], 0,
    'The Mediterranean climate is named after the Mediterranean Sea region (southern Europe) where it was first described. In South Africa, the Western Cape experiences this climate — dry, warm summers and cool, rainy winters. It is ideal for wine farming.'),
  t(3, '## Links Between Climate Regions and Factors\n\nEach climate region is the result of specific combinations of the factors we studied.\n\n### Tropical Climates\n\n- Found near the Equator (0°-23.5°)\n- Hot because the sun is nearly overhead all year\n- Wet because warm air holds more moisture and convection causes heavy rainfall\n- **Tropical rainforest**: Rain all year (no dry season) — e.g., Congo Basin\n- **Tropical savanna (grassland)**: Distinct wet and dry seasons — e.g., northern KwaZulu-Natal\n\n### Desert Climates\n\n- Found around 20°-30° latitude (the subtropical high-pressure belts)\n- Less than **250 mm** of rainfall per year\n- Large daily temperature range (very hot days, cold nights)\n- In South Africa: the **Kalahari** (semi-desert) and parts of the **Northern Cape**\n- The **Namib Desert** (along the west coast) is kept dry by the cold Benguela Current\n\n### The Mediterranean Climate of the Western Cape\n\nThe Western Cape is the only part of South Africa that receives **winter rainfall**:\n- In winter, cold fronts from the Atlantic bring rain\n- In summer, the South Atlantic high-pressure system pushes weather systems away\n- This climate supports the unique **fynbos** vegetation — one of the world\'s six floral kingdoms\n- The Mediterranean climate is ideal for growing **wine grapes**, fruit, and olives'),
  fb(4, 'Tropical climates are found near the ___, where the sun is nearly overhead all year. The Western Cape receives rain mainly in ___, unlike the rest of South Africa.',
    ['Equator', 'winter'],
    'Tropical regions receive the most direct sunlight, keeping temperatures high. The Western Cape\'s winter rainfall pattern is unique in South Africa — caused by cold fronts from the Atlantic that only penetrate the region in winter when the subtropical high-pressure belt shifts northward.'),
  t(5, '## Climate and Vegetation\n\nClimate determines what types of plants can grow in an area. South Africa has an extraordinary variety of vegetation due to its diverse climates.\n\n### South Africa\'s Biomes\n\n| Biome | Climate | Location | Key Features |\n|-------|---------|----------|---------------|\n| **Fynbos** | Mediterranean (winter rain) | Western Cape | Small-leaved shrubs; fire-adapted; extremely diverse |\n| **Savanna (bushveld)** | Hot, summer rainfall | Limpopo, NW, Mpumalanga, KZN | Grass with scattered trees; home to the Big Five |\n| **Grassland** | Temperate, summer rain | Highveld (Free State, Gauteng) | Dominated by grasses; cold winters with frost |\n| **Karoo** | Semi-arid | Interior (NC, EC, WC) | Drought-resistant shrubs; sparse vegetation |\n| **Forest** | Warm, high rainfall | Southern Cape, KZN coast | Dense trees; limited area in South Africa |\n| **Desert** | Very dry (<250 mm rain) | Namib, Richtersveld | Very sparse; drought-adapted succulents |\n| **Thicket** | Semi-arid to moderate | Eastern Cape | Dense, thorny shrubs; spekboomveld |\n\n### The Fynbos — A World Treasure\n\n- The Cape Floral Kingdom is the **smallest but most diverse** of the world\'s six floral kingdoms\n- It contains over **9 000 species**, most found nowhere else on Earth\n- Fynbos needs fire to regenerate — seeds germinate after burning\n- Protected in the **Cape Floral Region Protected Areas** (a UNESCO World Heritage Site)'),
  q(6, 'Why does the Western Cape have fynbos vegetation instead of grassland?',
    ['Fynbos is adapted to the Mediterranean climate with dry summers and wet winters, which is different from the grassland\'s summer rainfall pattern', 'Fynbos grows everywhere in South Africa', 'The government planted fynbos there', 'Grassland cannot grow near the ocean'], 0,
    'Fynbos has evolved to survive the Mediterranean climate\'s long dry summers and fire. Its plants have small, hard leaves (sclerophyllous) to reduce water loss. Grasslands need reliable summer rain, which the Western Cape does not receive.'),
  t(7, '## Climate Change and South Africa\n\nGlobal climate change is altering weather patterns worldwide, including in South Africa.\n\n### Evidence of Climate Change\n\n| Evidence | Detail |\n|----------|--------|\n| **Rising temperatures** | South Africa has warmed by about 1.5°C since 1931 — double the global average |\n| **Changing rainfall** | Western regions becoming drier; eastern regions may see more intense storms |\n| **Sea level rise** | Threatens coastal cities and infrastructure |\n| **Extreme weather** | More frequent droughts, floods, and heat waves |\n| **Melting ice** | Global ice caps and glaciers are shrinking |\n\n### Causes of Climate Change\n\n- The **greenhouse effect** is natural and keeps the Earth warm enough for life\n- Human activities have increased **greenhouse gases** (CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide) in the atmosphere\n- This enhanced greenhouse effect traps more heat, causing global warming\n- Main sources: burning fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas), deforestation, agriculture, industry\n\n### South Africa\'s Role\n\n- South Africa is the **14th largest emitter** of CO₂ in the world\n- Over **80% of electricity** comes from burning coal (Eskom power stations)\n- The country is transitioning to renewable energy (solar and wind)\n- South Africa signed the **Paris Agreement** (2015) committing to reduce emissions'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa has warmed by approximately ___°C since 1931. Over 80% of South Africa\'s electricity comes from burning ___.',
    ['1.5', 'coal'],
    'South Africa is warming at twice the global average rate, making it particularly vulnerable to climate change. The heavy dependence on coal for electricity makes South Africa one of the world\'s largest per-capita carbon emitters and a significant contributor to global warming.'),
  q(9, 'What is the difference between the natural greenhouse effect and enhanced greenhouse effect?',
    ['The natural greenhouse effect keeps Earth habitable, while the enhanced effect from human emissions causes dangerous additional warming', 'They are the same thing', 'The greenhouse effect only occurs in greenhouses', 'The natural greenhouse effect makes the Earth too cold'], 0,
    'The natural greenhouse effect is essential — without it, Earth would be about -18°C and uninhabitable. The enhanced greenhouse effect is the additional warming caused by human activities increasing greenhouse gases beyond natural levels. This extra warming is causing climate change.'),
  t(10, '## Climate and Human Activity\n\nClimate affects how people live, and human activities in turn affect climate.\n\n### How Climate Affects People in South Africa\n\n| Aspect | Climate Influence |\n|--------|-------------------|\n| **Agriculture** | What crops can be grown; when to plant and harvest |\n| **Water supply** | Rainfall determines water availability; droughts cause water crises |\n| **Settlement** | People tend to settle in areas with moderate climates and reliable water |\n| **Health** | Warm, wet climates harbour mosquitoes (malaria); heat waves cause health problems |\n| **Energy** | Cold winters increase heating demand; hot summers increase cooling demand |\n| **Tourism** | Climate attracts tourists — beaches (warm coast), snow (Drakensberg), wildlife (bushveld) |\n\n### Adapting to Climate Change\n\nSouth Africa must adapt to a changing climate:\n- **Water conservation**: Reducing waste, recycling water, harvesting rainwater\n- **Drought-resistant crops**: Developing crops that need less water\n- **Renewable energy**: Reducing dependence on coal\n- **Urban planning**: Designing cities to handle heat waves and flooding\n- **Disaster preparedness**: Being ready for extreme weather events\n\nUnderstanding climate is not just an academic exercise — it is essential for planning South Africa\'s future.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 5 (History – Term 3): The Scramble for Africa
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: European Colonisation of Africa in the Late 19th Century ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Impact of the Transatlantic Slave Trade on Africa\n\nBefore studying the scramble for Africa, we must understand the **transatlantic slave trade** and its lasting impact on the African continent.\n\n### The Transatlantic Slave Trade (1500s-1800s)\n\n- European powers — particularly Portugal, Spain, Britain, France, and the Netherlands — enslaved millions of Africans\n- Enslaved people were transported across the Atlantic to the Americas (the "Middle Passage")\n- Estimates: **12-15 million Africans** were shipped across the Atlantic; millions more died during capture and transport\n- The slave trade lasted over **300 years**\n\n### Impact on Africa\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Population loss** | Massive loss of young, healthy people — exactly the age group that produces food and builds communities |\n| **Warfare** | African kingdoms raided each other for captives to sell to European traders |\n| **Political instability** | Some kingdoms grew powerful through the slave trade; others were destroyed |\n| **Economic disruption** | Africa was drained of human capital and resources |\n| **Underdevelopment** | The slave trade contributed to Africa\'s long-term underdevelopment |\n\n### The Growth of Industrialisation in Europe\n\nProfits from the slave trade helped fund the Industrial Revolution in Europe. Ironically, industrialisation later made slavery less profitable, contributing to its abolition in the 19th century. But by then, the damage to Africa was immense.'),
  q(2, 'Approximately how many Africans were shipped across the Atlantic during the transatlantic slave trade?',
    ['12 to 15 million', '1 to 2 million', '100 million', '500 000'], 0,
    'Between the 1500s and 1800s, an estimated 12-15 million Africans were transported across the Atlantic in the transatlantic slave trade. Millions more died during capture, forced marches to the coast, and the horrific Middle Passage. The true toll is incalculable.'),
  t(3, '## European Colonisation of Africa\n\nIn the late 19th century, European powers rapidly conquered and divided the African continent among themselves. This process is known as the **scramble for Africa**.\n\n### Africa in the Late 19th Century\n\nBefore colonisation, Africa was home to hundreds of diverse societies:\n- Powerful kingdoms: Ashanti (Ghana), Zulu (South Africa), Ethiopia, Buganda (Uganda)\n- Trading empires connected to global commerce\n- Complex systems of governance, law, and culture\n- Rich natural resources\n\n### Why Did Europeans Colonise Africa?\n\n| Reason | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Economic** | Raw materials (rubber, palm oil, minerals, ivory) for European factories; markets to sell manufactured goods |\n| **Political** | National prestige and competition between European powers |\n| **Strategic** | Control of trade routes, ports, and the Suez Canal (opened 1869) |\n| **Technological** | Advances in weapons (machine guns), medicine (quinine for malaria), transport (steamships, railways) |\n| **Ideological** | "Civilising mission" — the racist belief that Europeans had a duty to "civilise" Africans |\n| **Missionary** | Spreading Christianity across Africa |\n\n### The "3 Cs"\n\nEuropean colonialism was often justified by the "3 Cs":\n- **Commerce**: Trade and economic exploitation\n- **Christianity**: Spreading European religion\n- **Civilisation**: Imposing European culture and values\n\nIn reality, colonisation was primarily about **power and profit**.'),
  fb(4, 'The rapid European conquest and division of Africa in the late 19th century is known as the ___ for Africa. Europeans justified colonisation using the "3 Cs": Commerce, Christianity, and ___.',
    ['scramble', 'Civilisation'],
    'The scramble for Africa saw European powers rapidly carve up the continent between roughly 1880 and 1914. While Europeans claimed to be spreading civilisation, the primary motives were economic exploitation and political competition between European nations.'),
  t(5, '## The Berlin Conference (1884-1885)\n\nThe **Berlin Conference** formalised the rules by which European powers would divide Africa among themselves.\n\n### Key Facts\n\n- Called by **Otto von Bismarck**, the German Chancellor\n- Held in **Berlin**, Germany, from November 1884 to February 1885\n- **14 European nations** attended, plus the United States\n- **No African leaders were invited** or consulted\n\n### Decisions Made at Berlin\n\n1. **Free trade zones**: The Congo and Niger river basins were opened to free trade for all European nations\n2. **Effective occupation**: A European power could only claim territory if it had a government, police, and treaties in the area — leading to a rush to occupy territory\n3. **Notification**: Powers had to notify others when claiming new territory\n4. **Abolition of the slave trade**: Officially declared (though enforcement was weak)\n\n### Impact of the Berlin Conference\n\n- Triggered the **rush to occupy** Africa — within 30 years, almost the entire continent was colonised\n- **Artificial borders** were drawn with straight lines on maps, dividing ethnic groups and combining rival peoples\n- African peoples, cultures, and political systems were ignored\n- These artificial borders remain today and continue to cause conflict\n\n### Who Colonised What?\n\n| Power | Main Territories |\n|-------|-------------------|\n| **Britain** | South Africa, Nigeria, Ghana, Kenya, Uganda, Sudan, Egypt |\n| **France** | West Africa (Senegal, Mali, etc.), Madagascar, Algeria |\n| **Germany** | Tanzania, Namibia (South West Africa), Cameroon, Togo |\n| **Belgium** | Congo (the "Congo Free State" under King Leopold II) |\n| **Portugal** | Mozambique, Angola, Guinea-Bissau |\n| **Italy** | Libya, Eritrea, Somalia |\n| **Spain** | Small territories in Morocco and Equatorial Guinea |'),
  q(6, 'What was significant about the Berlin Conference of 1884-1885?',
    ['European powers agreed on rules for dividing Africa among themselves, without inviting any African representatives', 'African leaders met to discuss European trade', 'It led to the independence of all African countries', 'It banned European colonisation of Africa'], 0,
    'The Berlin Conference is one of the most important events in African history. Fourteen European nations divided up an entire continent without consulting a single African leader. The borders they drew — often as straight lines on maps — remain the borders of most African countries today.'),
  t(7, '## Patterns and Results of Colonisation\n\n### How Europeans Conquered Africa\n\nEuropean conquest was possible because of **technological superiority**:\n\n| Advantage | How It Helped |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| **Machine guns (Maxim gun)** | Could mow down African armies armed with spears and older firearms |\n| **Steamships** | Allowed Europeans to navigate African rivers and transport troops |\n| **Railways** | Built to transport raw materials; also moved troops quickly |\n| **Quinine** | Treated malaria, allowing Europeans to survive in tropical Africa |\n| **Telegraph** | Rapid communication across vast distances |\n\n### Resistance to Colonisation\n\nAfricans did not accept colonisation passively. Examples of resistance:\n\n| Resistance | Location | Result |\n|------------|----------|--------|\n| **Battle of Adwa (1896)** | Ethiopia | Ethiopia defeated Italy — one of the few successful resistances |\n| **Ashanti wars** | Gold Coast (Ghana) | Ashanti fought the British repeatedly but were eventually conquered |\n| **Zulu resistance** | South Africa | Won at Isandlwana (1879) but ultimately defeated |\n| **Maji Maji Rebellion (1905-1907)** | Tanzania | Crushed by German forces with devastating consequences |\n| **Herero and Nama Genocide (1904-1908)** | Namibia | Germany committed genocide, killing 80% of Herero people |\n\n### Only Two African Countries Were Never Colonised\n\n- **Ethiopia**: Defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896 (though Italy invaded again in 1935-1941)\n- **Liberia**: Founded by freed American slaves; maintained independence under US protection'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ gun gave European armies a decisive military advantage over African forces. Ethiopia was one of only two African countries that was never permanently colonised, defeating Italy at the Battle of ___ in 1896.',
    ['Maxim', 'Adwa'],
    'The Maxim gun (an early machine gun) could fire 600 rounds per minute, giving small European forces devastating firepower against larger African armies. Ethiopia\'s victory at Adwa was a remarkable exception — Emperor Menelik II assembled a large, well-armed force that decisively defeated the Italian army.'),
  q(9, 'Why are the borders of many African countries straight lines?',
    ['European powers drew borders on maps based on their own interests, ignoring African ethnic, cultural, and linguistic boundaries', 'African leaders agreed to straight borders', 'Straight lines were the natural boundaries', 'Rivers in Africa always flow in straight lines'], 0,
    'European colonial borders were often drawn with rulers on maps in European capitals, following lines of latitude and longitude rather than natural or cultural boundaries. This divided ethnic groups across multiple countries and forced rival groups into the same territory — creating problems that continue today.'),
  t(10, '## Results of Colonisation for Africa\n\nColonisation had devastating and lasting effects on Africa.\n\n### Negative Effects\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Loss of independence** | African peoples lost control of their own lands and governance |\n| **Economic exploitation** | Resources were extracted for the benefit of European countries |\n| **Forced labour** | Africans were forced to work on plantations, in mines, and on infrastructure projects |\n| **Cultural destruction** | African languages, religions, and customs were suppressed |\n| **Artificial borders** | Ethnic groups divided; rival groups forced together |\n| **Land alienation** | The best farmland was taken by European settlers (especially in Kenya, Zimbabwe, South Africa) |\n| **Racism** | Racial hierarchies placed Europeans above Africans |\n\n### The Congo Free State: A Case of Extreme Exploitation\n\n- King **Leopold II of Belgium** personally owned the Congo as his private property (1885-1908)\n- Congolese people were forced to collect **rubber** under a system of terror\n- Those who did not meet quotas had their **hands cut off**\n- An estimated **10 million people died** under Leopold\'s rule\n- International outcry eventually forced Belgium to take over the colony\n\n### Long-Term Effects\n\n- Colonial economies were designed to **export raw materials** and **import manufactured goods** — this pattern persists\n- Colonial education systems produced elites who identified with European culture\n- Colonial boundaries continue to cause ethnic conflict\n- The psychological damage of racism and colonial domination took generations to heal\n- These effects are still visible in Africa today'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Case Study — The Ashanti Kingdom ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Ashanti Kingdom\n\nThe **Ashanti (Asante) Kingdom** was one of the most powerful and sophisticated states in West Africa. It is located in what is now **Ghana**, in the region called the **Gold Coast** by Europeans.\n\n### Origins and Rise of the Ashanti\n\n- The Ashanti Kingdom was founded around **1670** by **Osei Tutu**, who united several Akan-speaking chieftaincies\n- The symbol of Ashanti unity was the **Golden Stool** (*Sika Dwa Kofi*), believed to contain the soul of the Ashanti nation\n- The capital was **Kumasi**, a large and well-organised city\n\n### Political Organisation\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Ruler** | The **Asantehene** (king) — supreme political and military leader |\n| **Council** | A council of chiefs advised the Asantehene and could remove an incompetent king |\n| **Provinces** | The kingdom was divided into provinces, each governed by a chief |\n| **Administration** | A sophisticated bureaucracy collected taxes and administered justice |\n| **Military** | A large, well-organised army with divisions and commanders |\n| **Diplomacy** | The Ashanti conducted diplomacy with both African states and European powers |\n\n### The Economy\n\n- The Ashanti Kingdom controlled the **gold trade** — the Gold Coast was one of the world\'s major gold-producing regions\n- Other trade goods: kola nuts, slaves, cloth, and foodstuffs\n- Trade routes connected Kumasi to the coast and to trans-Saharan trade networks\n- The Ashanti were skilled metalworkers, weavers (famous **kente cloth**), and woodcarvers'),
  q(2, 'What was the Golden Stool and why was it important to the Ashanti people?',
    ['It was the symbol of Ashanti unity, believed to contain the soul of the nation — it represented the Ashanti identity', 'It was an ordinary chair used by the king', 'It was a piece of furniture made of actual gold to display wealth', 'It was a gift from the British'], 0,
    'The Golden Stool (*Sika Dwa Kofi*) was the most sacred symbol of the Ashanti nation. According to tradition, the priest Okomfo Anokye conjured it from the sky and it descended onto King Osei Tutu\'s lap. It was never sat upon — it symbolised the unity and soul of the entire Ashanti people.'),
  t(3, '## European Contact and the Gold Coast\n\n### Early European Contact\n\n- The Portuguese were the first Europeans to reach the Gold Coast in the **1470s**\n- They built **Elmina Castle** (1482) — originally a trading post for gold\n- Other European nations followed: the Dutch, British, Danish, and Swedish\n- European forts along the coast traded gold, ivory, and eventually **enslaved people**\n\n### The Transatlantic Slave Trade and the Ashanti\n\n- The slave trade had a complex impact on the Ashanti:\n  - The Ashanti participated in the slave trade, selling war captives and criminals to European traders\n  - Profits from the slave trade increased Ashanti military power and territorial expansion\n  - But the slave trade also created conflict and instability in the region\n  - After Britain abolished the slave trade (1807) and slavery (1833), the relationship between Britain and the Ashanti changed\n\n### British Interest in the Gold Coast\n\n- After abolishing slavery, Britain became more interested in **legitimate trade** (palm oil, gold, timber)\n- Britain gradually took control of coastal areas from other European powers\n- Conflict with the Ashanti was inevitable as both wanted to control trade and territory'),
  fb(4, '___ Castle, built by the Portuguese in 1482, was one of the first European trading posts on the Gold Coast. The British abolished the slave trade in ___, changing the relationship between Britain and the Ashanti.',
    ['Elmina', '1807'],
    'Elmina Castle is one of the oldest European structures in sub-Saharan Africa. Originally built for gold trading, it later became a major slave-trading fort. Britain\'s abolition of the slave trade in 1807 shifted its focus to other forms of commerce and eventually to colonial conquest.'),
  t(5, '## The Anglo-Ashanti Wars\n\nThe British and the Ashanti fought a series of wars over nearly a century as Britain tried to expand its control inland from the coast.\n\n### The Wars\n\n| War | Date | Key Events | Result |\n|-----|------|------------|--------|\n| **First** | 1824-1831 | Ashanti invaded the coast; British governor Sir Charles MacCarthy was killed | Ashanti withdrew after initial victories |\n| **Second** | 1863-1864 | Ashanti raided the coast | Inconclusive |\n| **Third** | 1873-1874 | British expedition under Sir Garnet Wolseley | British burned Kumasi; Ashanti signed a treaty |\n| **Fourth** | 1895-1896 | Britain demanded Ashanti submit to "protectorate" status | Ashanti king (Prempeh I) exiled; kingdom weakened |\n| **War of the Golden Stool** | 1900 | Governor Hodgson demanded the Golden Stool; Ashanti rose in revolt, led by **Yaa Asantewaa** (Queen Mother) | Ashanti resistance ultimately defeated |\n\n### Why Did the Ashanti Resist So Strongly?\n\n1. **Pride and sovereignty**: The Ashanti had a long history of independence\n2. **The Golden Stool**: The British demand for the Golden Stool was a direct attack on Ashanti identity\n3. **Military tradition**: The Ashanti had a powerful, well-organised army\n4. **Economic interests**: The Ashanti wanted to control trade on their own terms'),
  q(6, 'What triggered the War of the Golden Stool in 1900?',
    ['The British governor demanded that the Ashanti hand over the Golden Stool so he could sit on it, which was a massive insult to Ashanti identity', 'The Ashanti attacked British traders', 'Britain discovered gold in Kumasi', 'The Ashanti king invited the British to war'], 0,
    'In 1900, British Governor Sir Frederick Hodgson demanded the Golden Stool, saying he wanted to sit on it. This was an extraordinary insult — even the Asantehene never sat on the Golden Stool. It galvanised Ashanti resistance, led by the warrior queen Yaa Asantewaa.'),
  t(7, '## Yaa Asantewaa and Ashanti Resistance\n\n**Yaa Asantewaa** (c. 1840-1921) was the Queen Mother of the Ashanti state of Ejisu. She is one of the most celebrated figures in Ghanaian and African history.\n\n### The Rebellion\n\n- When the British demanded the Golden Stool and many Ashanti chiefs hesitated to fight, Yaa Asantewaa stood up:\n- She declared: *"If you, the men of Ashanti, will not go forward, then we will. I shall call upon my fellow women. We will fight the white men. We will fight till the last of us falls in the battlefields."*\n- She led the Ashanti forces in a **siege of the British fort at Kumasi** that lasted several months\n- The British were trapped and nearly starved before reinforcements arrived\n\n### The Aftermath\n\n- British reinforcements eventually defeated the Ashanti\n- Yaa Asantewaa was captured and **exiled** to the Seychelles (like King Prempeh I before her)\n- She died in exile in **1921** at approximately 80 years old\n- The Golden Stool was **hidden** and was never surrendered to the British\n\n### Legacy\n\n- The Ashanti eventually came under British colonial rule as part of the Gold Coast colony\n- But the Golden Stool was preserved — it was found accidentally in 1920 and returned to the Ashanti\n- Yaa Asantewaa is a symbol of **African resistance** to colonisation and of women\'s leadership in the struggle\n- Ghana gained independence in **1957** under **Kwame Nkrumah** — the first sub-Saharan African country to do so'),
  fb(8, 'Yaa ___ was the Queen Mother who led Ashanti resistance against the British in 1900. The Gold Coast (including the Ashanti region) became the independent nation of ___ in 1957.',
    ['Asantewaa', 'Ghana'],
    'Yaa Asantewaa is celebrated as one of Africa\'s greatest resistance leaders. Her courage in leading the fight against British colonialism, at a time when many male chiefs hesitated, makes her an icon of both anti-colonial resistance and women\'s empowerment.'),
  q(9, 'What happened to the Golden Stool?',
    ['It was hidden by the Ashanti and never surrendered to the British, eventually being returned to the Ashanti people', 'The British took it to London', 'It was destroyed in the war', 'It was melted down for its gold'], 0,
    'The Golden Stool was hidden by loyal Ashanti subjects and was never captured by the British. When it was accidentally discovered in 1920, it was returned to the Ashanti royal family. It remains in Kumasi today and is still a powerful symbol of Ashanti identity and unity.'),
  t(10, '## The Results of Colonisation for the Ashanti Kingdom\n\nThe colonisation of the Ashanti Kingdom illustrates the broader patterns of European colonisation across Africa.\n\n### What Changed Under Colonial Rule\n\n| Before Colonisation | Under British Rule |\n|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| Ashanti self-governance | British colonial administration |\n| The Asantehene ruled | British governor controlled policy |\n| Ashanti controlled their own trade | Britain controlled trade and set prices |\n| Gold wealth stayed in Ashanti | Gold profits went to Britain |\n| Ashanti culture and customs | European culture imposed through schools and churches |\n| Ashanti military independence | Ashanti army disbanded |\n\n### Broader Lessons\n\n1. **African societies were not primitive** — the Ashanti Kingdom was a sophisticated, well-organised state\n2. **Resistance was widespread** — Africans fought colonisation with courage and determination\n3. **Colonisation was driven by greed**, not by any desire to "help" Africans\n4. **Cultural destruction** accompanied political and economic conquest\n5. **The effects of colonisation persist** — artificial borders, economic patterns, and social divisions created during colonialism continue to shape Africa today\n\n### Connection to South Africa\n\nThe scramble for Africa explains why South Africa was colonised by Britain. British interest in South Africa intensified with the mineral revolution — just as it was the gold of the Gold Coast that attracted Britain to the Ashanti Kingdom. The pattern of colonisation — conquest, exploitation, racial hierarchy — was remarkably similar across the continent.'),
];

