// --- Lesson 1: Rural and Urban Settlements ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is a Settlement?\n\nA **settlement** is a place where people live. Settlements range from tiny isolated farmsteads to huge cities like Johannesburg.\n\n### Types of Settlements\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Isolated dwelling** | A single house or farm, far from others | A farm in the Karoo |\n| **Hamlet** | A few houses grouped together | A small cluster in rural Limpopo |\n| **Village** | A small settlement with basic services (shop, school, church) | A village in the Eastern Cape |\n| **Town** | A larger settlement with more services and economic activities | Graaff-Reinet, Mossel Bay |\n| **City** | A large settlement with many services, industries, and a big population | Cape Town, Durban |\n| **Metropolis** | A very large city, often with surrounding suburbs | Johannesburg, eThekwini |\n| **Megalopolis** | A continuous urban area formed by several cities merging | Johannesburg-Pretoria (Gauteng) |\n\n### Rural vs Urban Settlements\n\n| Feature | Rural | Urban |\n|---------|-------|--------|\n| **Population** | Small | Large |\n| **Density** | Low (spread out) | High (densely packed) |\n| **Economy** | Farming, fishing, mining | Industry, services, trade |\n| **Services** | Limited | Many (hospitals, schools, shops) |\n| **Infrastructure** | Basic (dirt roads, limited water/electricity) | Developed (tarred roads, piped water, electricity grid) |\n| **Land use** | Mostly open/agricultural | Built-up areas, commercial, industrial |'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between a rural and an urban settlement?',
    ['Rural settlements are small with low population density and farming-based economies, while urban settlements are large, dense, and based on industry and services', 'Rural areas have more people', 'Urban areas are always older than rural areas', 'There is no difference between rural and urban'], 0,
    'Rural settlements are characterised by low population density, dependence on agriculture or primary activities, and limited services. Urban settlements are densely populated with diverse economic activities (industry, commerce, services) and well-developed infrastructure.'),
  t(3, '## Rural Settlements in South Africa\n\n### Types of Rural Settlements\n\n| Pattern | Description | Example |\n|---------|-------------|----------|\n| **Isolated farmsteads** | Single farms scattered across the landscape | Sheep farms in the Karoo |\n| **Linear settlement** | Houses arranged along a road, river, or coastline | Fishing villages along the coast |\n| **Nucleated (clustered) settlement** | Houses grouped around a central point (crossroads, water source, church) | Many former mission stations |\n| **Dispersed (scattered) settlement** | Houses spread out with no clear pattern | Rural areas in KwaZulu-Natal |\n\n### Economic Activities in Rural Areas\n\n- **Farming**: Subsistence farming (growing food for the family) and commercial farming (growing for sale)\n- **Forestry**: Timber plantations in Mpumalanga and KwaZulu-Natal\n- **Fishing**: Along the coasts and in some inland areas\n- **Mining**: Some mines are in rural areas (e.g., coal mines in Mpumalanga)\n\n### Challenges in Rural South Africa\n\n| Challenge | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Poverty** | Rural areas have the highest poverty rates in South Africa |\n| **Limited services** | Many villages lack clean water, electricity, and good schools |\n| **Unemployment** | Very few job opportunities outside of farming |\n| **Migration** | Young people leave for cities, creating an ageing rural population |\n| **Land issues** | Unresolved land claims; inadequate land for farming |\n| **Distance** | Far from hospitals, shops, and government offices |'),
  fb(4, 'A settlement where houses are arranged along a road or river is called a ___ settlement. A settlement where houses are grouped around a central point is called a ___ settlement.',
    ['linear', 'nucleated'],
    'Linear settlements develop along transport routes (roads, rivers) or geographical features (coastlines, valleys). Nucleated settlements develop around focal points like markets, water sources, crossroads, or institutions like churches or schools.'),
  t(5, '## Urban Settlements\n\n### Urban Settlement Patterns in South Africa\n\nSouth African cities have unique characteristics shaped by history, particularly **apartheid**.\n\n### The Apartheid City Model\n\nDuring apartheid, the **Group Areas Act (1950)** forced different racial groups into separate residential areas:\n\n| Zone | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **CBD (Central Business District)** | Commercial centre — shops, offices, banks |\n| **White suburbs** | Closest to the CBD; best services; low density; large houses |\n| **Indian/Coloured areas** | Buffer zones between white and black areas |\n| **Black townships** | Furthest from the CBD; poorest services; high density |\n| **Industrial areas** | Factories, often located between white and black areas |\n| **Buffer zones** | Open land, highways, or railways separating racial areas |\n\n### Post-Apartheid Changes\n\n- People are now free to live where they can afford\n- But the apartheid city structure largely **persists** because of economic inequality\n- Informal settlements have grown on the edges of cities\n- New housing developments (RDP houses) have been built but are often far from economic centres\n- **Desegregation** has occurred mainly in formerly white suburbs (now more diverse) and in the CBD'),
  q(6, 'Why does the spatial pattern of apartheid cities still persist in South Africa today?',
    ['Economic inequality means most black South Africans cannot afford to move to formerly white areas, so the racial geography largely remains', 'Apartheid laws are still in place', 'People prefer to live separately', 'The government prevents people from moving'], 0,
    'Although apartheid laws were abolished, the economic inequality created by decades of apartheid means most people of colour still live in townships and informal settlements. Property prices in formerly white areas remain out of reach for most. Breaking the apartheid city structure requires addressing deep economic inequality.'),
  t(7, '## Land Use in Urban Areas\n\nCities have different zones used for different purposes.\n\n### Urban Land Use Zones\n\n| Zone | Function | Features |\n|------|----------|----------|\n| **CBD (Central Business District)** | Commerce and business | Tall buildings, offices, shops, banks; highest land values |\n| **Residential** | Housing | Ranges from low-density suburbs to high-density flats and townships |\n| **Industrial** | Manufacturing and production | Factories, warehouses; often near railways or highways |\n| **Commercial** | Shopping and services | Shopping centres, malls, restaurants |\n| **Recreational** | Parks, sports, leisure | Parks, stadiums, nature reserves |\n| **Transport** | Movement of people and goods | Roads, railways, bus stations, airports |\n\n### Reading Land Use on Maps and Aerial Photographs\n\nOn aerial photographs and satellite images, you can identify land use by:\n- **Regular grid pattern**: Residential areas\n- **Large rectangular buildings with parking**: Commercial/industrial\n- **Green open spaces**: Parks or sports fields\n- **Dense, irregular structures**: Informal settlements\n- **Wide grey/black lines**: Major roads or highways\n- **Railway lines**: Straight lines with branching sidings near industrial areas'),
  fb(8, 'The area of a city where shops, offices, and banks are concentrated is called the ___ (Central Business District). On aerial photographs, informal settlements appear as ___, irregular structures.',
    ['CBD', 'dense'],
    'The CBD is the commercial heart of the city with the highest land values. Informal settlements are identifiable on aerial photographs by their dense, irregularly arranged structures, often without formal roads or planned layout.'),
  q(9, 'What is the typical arrangement of urban land use zones in a South African city?',
    ['The CBD is at the centre, surrounded by commercial and industrial areas, with residential areas (suburbs and townships) on the outskirts', 'Residential areas are always in the centre', 'Industrial areas are always in the CBD', 'There is no pattern to urban land use'], 0,
    'South African cities typically follow a pattern with the CBD at the centre, surrounded by older residential and commercial areas. Industrial zones are often between residential areas, and newer residential areas (including townships and informal settlements) are on the outskirts.'),
  t(10, '## Identifying Land Use on Aerial Photographs and Large-Scale Maps\n\nGeographers use aerial photographs and large-scale maps to study and plan urban settlements.\n\n### Aerial Photographs: Oblique vs Vertical\n\n| Type | Angle | Use |\n|------|-------|------|\n| **Oblique** | Taken at an angle from the side | Shows buildings from the side; easier to recognise features |\n| **Vertical** | Taken from directly above (looking straight down) | Used for mapping; features appear as "plan view" from above |\n\n### What You Can Identify\n\n| Feature | Appearance on Vertical Photo |\n|---------|------------------------------|\n| **High-income residential** | Large houses with gardens, swimming pools, wide streets |\n| **Low-income residential** | Small houses, narrow streets, high density |\n| **Informal settlement** | Very dense, irregular layout, no gardens |\n| **Industrial area** | Large rectangular buildings, loading bays, parking lots |\n| **Sports fields** | Green rectangular or oval shapes with markings |\n| **Schools** | Buildings with open grounds and sports fields |\n| **Roads** | Grey/black lines; highways are wider with interchanges |\n\n### Why This Skill Matters\n\n- **Urban planners** use these skills to plan new developments\n- **Environmental managers** identify problem areas (erosion, pollution, deforestation)\n- **Government officials** plan service delivery (water, electricity, roads)\n- **Students** need these skills for Social Sciences assessments and examinations'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Urbanisation ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Urbanisation?\n\n**Urbanisation** is the process by which an increasing proportion of a country\'s population lives in urban areas (cities and towns).\n\n### Urbanisation in Numbers\n\n| Statistic | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **World** | Over 55% of the world\'s population now lives in urban areas (2023) |\n| **South Africa** | About 68% of South Africans live in urban areas |\n| **Africa** | The most rapidly urbanising continent — urban population expected to double by 2050 |\n| **Rate** | In 1950, only about 30% of the world was urbanised; by 2050, it will be about 70% |\n\n### Push and Pull Factors\n\nPeople move from rural areas to cities for many reasons:\n\n| Push Factors (leaving rural areas) | Pull Factors (attracted to cities) |\n|---------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Poverty and unemployment | Job opportunities |\n| Limited services (schools, hospitals) | Better education and healthcare |\n| Drought and poor harvests | Higher wages |\n| Lack of electricity and clean water | Electricity, water, and other services |\n| Boredom and limited social life | Entertainment and social opportunities |\n| Natural disasters | Better housing (perceived) |'),
  q(2, 'What is urbanisation?',
    ['The process of an increasing proportion of a country\'s population moving to and living in cities', 'The process of building new roads in rural areas', 'People moving from cities to rural areas', 'The growth of farms around cities'], 0,
    'Urbanisation refers to the shift from rural to urban living. In South Africa, urbanisation has been driven by people seeking employment, education, and services in cities. About 68% of South Africans now live in urban areas.'),
  t(3, '## Urbanisation in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa\'s urbanisation has been shaped by its unique history of colonialism and apartheid.\n\n### Historical Context\n\n| Period | Urbanisation Pattern |\n|--------|---------------------|\n| **Pre-colonial** | Most people lived in rural settlements |\n| **Colonial (1800s)** | Mining towns (Kimberley, Johannesburg) attracted workers |\n| **Apartheid (1948-1994)** | Influx control laws restricted black urbanisation; people were forced to live in "homelands" and townships |\n| **Post-apartheid (1994+)** | Removal of influx control; rapid urban migration; growth of informal settlements |\n\n### Migration in South Africa\n\n- After 1994, the removal of apartheid laws allowing free movement led to **rapid urbanisation**\n- Gauteng receives the most migrants (from all provinces and neighbouring countries)\n- Cities in the Western Cape (Cape Town) and KwaZulu-Natal (Durban) also attract many migrants\n- Migration from neighbouring countries (Zimbabwe, Mozambique, Lesotho, Nigeria) adds to urban growth\n\n### Review From Grade 6: "Why People Live Where They Do"\n\n- **Resources**: Water, fertile soil, minerals\n- **Climate**: Comfortable temperatures, reliable rainfall\n- **Economic opportunities**: Jobs, trade, markets\n- **Services**: Schools, hospitals, transport\n- **History**: Colonial settlements, mining towns, railway junctions'),
  fb(4, 'During apartheid, ___ control laws restricted the movement of black South Africans into cities. After 1994, the province receiving the most migrants has been ___.',
    ['influx', 'Gauteng'],
    'Influx control was a system of pass laws and regulations designed to prevent black South Africans from moving permanently to cities. After these laws were abolished, Gauteng (Johannesburg and Pretoria) became the primary destination for migrants seeking employment and opportunities.'),
  t(5, '## Problems of Rapid Urbanisation\n\nRapid urbanisation creates serious challenges, especially in developing countries like South Africa.\n\n### Housing\n\n- The housing backlog in South Africa is estimated at over **2 million houses**\n- Many people live in **informal settlements** (shacks made of corrugated iron, wood, and plastic)\n- The government has built over 4 million RDP/BNG houses since 1994, but demand outstrips supply\n- Informal settlements often lack water, electricity, sanitation, and paved roads\n\n### Service Delivery\n\n| Service | Challenge |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Water** | Many informal settlements rely on communal taps or have no clean water |\n| **Sanitation** | Inadequate toilets; raw sewage in some areas |\n| **Electricity** | Illegal connections are common and dangerous |\n| **Waste removal** | Irregular or non-existent in informal settlements |\n| **Roads** | Unpaved, flooding during rains |\n\n### Other Problems\n\n- **Unemployment**: Cities cannot create jobs fast enough for new arrivals\n- **Crime**: Poverty and inequality contribute to high crime rates\n- **Congestion**: Overcrowded roads and inadequate public transport\n- **Pollution**: Air and water pollution from industry, vehicles, and waste\n- **Healthcare**: Overcrowded clinics and hospitals'),
  q(6, 'Why have informal settlements grown rapidly in South African cities since 1994?',
    ['The removal of apartheid movement restrictions led to rapid urban migration, but housing construction cannot keep pace with demand', 'The government built informal settlements', 'People prefer living in informal settlements', 'Informal settlements existed before 1994 and have not grown'], 0,
    'When apartheid\'s influx control laws were removed, millions of people moved to cities seeking better lives. But the government\'s housing programme — despite building over 4 million houses — cannot keep up with demand. The gap between housing supply and demand results in informal settlements.'),
  t(7, '## Social Issues Related to Urbanisation\n\nUrbanisation creates and intensifies social challenges in South African cities.\n\n### Housing and Service Provision\n\nThe government\'s response to the housing crisis includes:\n- **RDP houses**: Government-subsidised houses for low-income families (small but formal)\n- **BNG (Breaking New Ground)**: Improved policy providing better-quality housing and mixed-income developments\n- **Social housing**: Rental housing managed by housing institutions\n- **Sites and services**: Government provides serviced plots (water, electricity, sanitation) for people to build their own homes\n\n### Healthcare and Education\n\n- Urban areas have better healthcare facilities, but they are often overcrowded\n- Schools in townships and informal settlements are often under-resourced\n- The gap between well-resourced former "Model C" schools and township schools remains wide\n\n### The Apartheid Legacy\n\nThe apartheid city structure continues to create problems:\n- Most black South Africans live far from economic centres\n- Long commutes increase transport costs and reduce quality of life\n- Racial and economic segregation persists despite legal desegregation\n- **Spatial inequality**: The physical layout of cities reinforces economic inequality'),
  fb(8, 'Government-subsidised housing for low-income families is commonly known as ___ houses. The physical layout of South African cities that reinforces economic inequality is called ___ inequality.',
    ['RDP', 'spatial'],
    'RDP (Reconstruction and Development Programme) houses are small, subsidised houses built by the government for qualifying low-income families. Spatial inequality refers to the way the physical layout of cities — a legacy of apartheid planning — means poorer communities are far from jobs, services, and opportunities.'),
  q(9, 'Why are long commutes a major problem for residents of South African townships?',
    ['Townships were built far from city centres and workplaces, so residents spend significant time and money on transport', 'Most township residents work from home', 'Transport in South Africa is free', 'Commuting is only a problem in rural areas'], 0,
    'Under apartheid, townships were deliberately located far from white areas and economic centres. This means many black South Africans still face long daily commutes (often 2-4 hours round trip), spending a large proportion of their income on taxis, buses, and trains.'),
  t(10, '## Solutions to Urbanisation Challenges\n\nAddressing the problems of urbanisation requires a multi-pronged approach.\n\n### Government Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Goal |\n|----------|------|\n| **Integrated housing developments** | Mixed-income communities closer to jobs and services |\n| **Upgrading informal settlements** | Providing basic services (water, electricity, sanitation) in existing settlements |\n| **Public transport investment** | BRT systems (Rea Vaya, MyCiTi), Gautrain, Metrorail improvements |\n| **Urban renewal** | Revitalising inner-city areas to create mixed-use neighbourhoods |\n| **Rural development** | Investing in rural areas to reduce the push to cities |\n\n### Individual and Community Actions\n\n- **Community participation**: Residents participating in local government planning\n- **Urban agriculture**: Growing food in cities to improve food security\n- **Waste recycling**: Creating jobs and reducing waste\n- **Skills development**: Training programmes to improve employment prospects\n\n### The Ideal\n\nA well-planned city should:\n- Provide affordable housing near workplaces\n- Have efficient public transport\n- Offer quality services (water, electricity, sanitation) to all\n- Include green spaces for recreation and environmental health\n- Create economic opportunities for all residents\n- Be safe and inclusive\n\nAchieving this vision requires sustained investment, good governance, and community participation — a challenge that South Africa continues to grapple with.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 7 (History – Term 4): World War I (1914-1918)
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Reasons Why World War I Broke Out ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Background to World War I\n\nWorld War I (1914-1918) was one of the most devastating conflicts in history. It was called the **"Great War"** because no previous war had been so destructive.\n\n### Scale of the War\n\n- Over **70 million military personnel** were mobilised worldwide\n- Approximately **9-10 million soldiers** were killed\n- An estimated **7 million civilians** died\n- The war involved countries from every continent\n- It was the first truly **global** war\n\n### The Main Players\n\n| Alliance | Countries |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Allied Powers (Entente)** | Britain, France, Russia (later joined by Italy, USA, Japan, and others) |\n| **Central Powers** | Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire (Turkey), Bulgaria |\n\n### Long-Term Causes\n\nWorld War I did not happen suddenly. It was the result of decades of building tensions in Europe, often summarised as **MAIN**:\n- **M** = Militarism\n- **A** = Alliance systems\n- **I** = Imperialism\n- **N** = Nationalism'),
  q(2, 'What does the acronym MAIN stand for in relation to the causes of World War I?',
    ['Militarism, Alliance systems, Imperialism, and Nationalism', 'Money, Arms, Industry, and Nations', 'Military, Army, Infantry, and Navy', 'Maps, Alliances, Intelligence, and Negotiation'], 0,
    'MAIN is a helpful way to remember the four long-term causes of World War I. These factors — Militarism, Alliances, Imperialism, and Nationalism — created a powder keg of tensions in Europe that one spark (the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand) would ignite.'),
  t(3, '## Nationalism\n\n**Nationalism** is an intense pride in and loyalty to one\'s nation or ethnic group, and the belief that your nation is superior to others.\n\n### How Nationalism Caused Tension\n\n- European countries competed for power, territory, and prestige\n- Each nation believed it was the greatest and deserved to dominate\n- Nationalism led to **rivalry** between major powers, especially between:\n  - **Britain and Germany**: Competing for industrial and naval supremacy\n  - **France and Germany**: France wanted revenge for losing Alsace-Lorraine in the Franco-Prussian War (1870-1871)\n  - **Russia and Austria-Hungary**: Both wanted influence in the Balkans\n\n### Nationalism in the Balkans\n\nThe Balkans (southeastern Europe) were called the **"powder keg of Europe"**:\n- The Ottoman Empire was collapsing, leaving a power vacuum\n- Small nations (Serbia, Bulgaria, Greece, Romania) wanted independence\n- **Serbia** wanted to unite all South Slavic peoples — this threatened **Austria-Hungary**, which ruled over many Slavic peoples\n- Russia supported Serbia (they were both Slavic nations)\n- Austria-Hungary saw Serbian nationalism as a threat to its multi-ethnic empire\n\nThis volatile mix of nationalism in the Balkans would provide the **spark** that started the war.'),
  fb(4, 'The Balkans were called the "___ keg of Europe" because of intense nationalist tensions. ___ wanted to unite all South Slavic peoples, which threatened Austria-Hungary.',
    ['powder', 'Serbia'],
    'The Balkans were incredibly volatile because of overlapping nationalist claims, the collapsing Ottoman Empire, and the rivalry between Austria-Hungary and Russia for influence in the region. Serbian nationalism directly threatened Austria-Hungary\'s multi-ethnic empire.'),
  t(5, '## Imperialism, Militarism, and Alliances\n\n### Imperialism\n\n- European powers competed fiercely for **colonies** in Africa, Asia, and the Pacific\n- Colonies provided raw materials, markets, and national prestige\n- **Competition over colonies** created tensions between European powers:\n  - Britain and France nearly went to war over Fashoda (Sudan) in 1898\n  - Germany and France clashed over Morocco in 1905 and 1911\n- The scramble for Africa intensified European rivalries\n\n### Militarism\n\n- European powers engaged in a massive **arms race**, building up armies and navies\n- **Germany** built a powerful navy to challenge Britain\'s naval supremacy\n- Britain responded with the **HMS Dreadnought** (1906) — a revolutionary battleship\n- By 1914, the major powers had **millions of soldiers** trained and ready\n- Military leaders developed war plans (e.g., Germany\'s **Schlieffen Plan**) — making war seem inevitable\n\n### The Alliance Systems\n\nEurope was divided into two hostile alliances:\n\n| Alliance | Members | Formed |\n|----------|---------|--------|\n| **Triple Alliance** | Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy | 1882 |\n| **Triple Entente** | Britain, France, Russia | 1907 |\n\nThese alliances meant that a conflict between **two countries** could quickly drag in **all the major powers** — which is exactly what happened.'),
  q(6, 'How did the alliance system contribute to the outbreak of World War I?',
    ['It meant that a conflict between two countries would automatically involve all the major powers, turning a local dispute into a world war', 'It prevented all wars in Europe', 'It only involved two countries', 'Alliances made countries weaker'], 0,
    'The alliance system created a domino effect. When Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia, Russia mobilised to support Serbia. Germany then declared war on Russia (and France). Britain entered because Germany invaded Belgium. What started as a local Balkan conflict became a world war within a week.'),
  t(7, '## The Immediate Cause: The Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand\n\nThe **spark** that ignited World War I was the assassination of the heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne.\n\n### The Event\n\n- On **28 June 1914**, **Archduke Franz Ferdinand** of Austria-Hungary and his wife Sophie visited **Sarajevo**, the capital of Bosnia (part of Austria-Hungary)\n- A group of Serbian nationalists called the **Black Hand** plotted to assassinate him\n- After an initial failed bomb attempt, a 19-year-old **Gavrilo Princip** shot and killed both Franz Ferdinand and Sophie\n\n### The Chain Reaction\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **28 June** | Franz Ferdinand assassinated in Sarajevo |\n| **28 July** | Austria-Hungary declares war on Serbia |\n| **30 July** | Russia mobilises its army to support Serbia |\n| **1 August** | Germany declares war on Russia |\n| **3 August** | Germany declares war on France; invades Belgium |\n| **4 August** | Britain declares war on Germany (because Britain had guaranteed Belgian neutrality) |\n\n### Why the Assassination Led to World War\n\n- Austria-Hungary blamed Serbia for the assassination\n- Austria-Hungary issued an ultimatum with harsh demands; Serbia rejected some of them\n- The alliance system pulled in one country after another\n- Military plans (like the Schlieffen Plan) required rapid mobilisation, leaving no time for diplomacy\n- Within **six weeks**, most of Europe was at war'),
  fb(8, 'Archduke Franz ___ was assassinated in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 by Gavrilo Princip. Britain entered the war because Germany invaded ___, whose neutrality Britain had guaranteed.',
    ['Ferdinand', 'Belgium'],
    'The assassination of Franz Ferdinand was the immediate trigger for WWI. Each country\'s alliances and military plans created a chain reaction that turned a regional crisis into a global war within weeks. Britain joined because Germany violated Belgian neutrality.'),
  q(9, 'Who assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand and what was his motivation?',
    ['Gavrilo Princip, a Serbian nationalist who wanted to free South Slavic peoples from Austro-Hungarian rule', 'A German soldier who wanted to start a war', 'A French spy sent to weaken Austria-Hungary', 'An Italian anarchist opposed to all monarchies'], 0,
    'Gavrilo Princip was a 19-year-old Bosnian Serb who was part of the Black Hand, a Serbian nationalist organisation. He believed that killing the Austrian heir would help liberate South Slavic peoples from Austro-Hungarian domination. Instead, it triggered a catastrophic world war.'),
  t(10, '## Control of the Seas\n\nBritain\'s control of the seas was a crucial factor both before and during World War I.\n\n### Britain\'s Naval Supremacy\n\n- Britain had the world\'s largest navy — essential for protecting its global empire and trade routes\n- The **Royal Navy** controlled the sea lanes that connected Britain to its colonies\n- Germany\'s decision to build a large navy (under Kaiser Wilhelm II) directly threatened Britain\n\n### The Naval Arms Race\n\n| Event | Date | Significance |\n|-------|------|---------------|\n| Germany begins naval expansion | 1898 | Challenges British naval supremacy |\n| HMS Dreadnought launched | 1906 | Revolutionary battleship — made all others obsolete |\n| Germany builds its own dreadnoughts | 1907+ | Arms race intensifies |\n| By 1914 | — | Britain: 29 dreadnoughts; Germany: 17 |\n\n### Why This Mattered\n\n- Britain saw German naval expansion as a **direct threat** to its security and empire\n- The naval race increased **mistrust** and hostility between Britain and Germany\n- When war broke out, the British navy **blockaded** Germany, cutting off its food and raw material imports\n- This blockade slowly strangled the German economy and contributed to Germany\'s defeat\n- The **Battle of Jutland (1916)** was the only major naval battle — inconclusive, but the German fleet never seriously challenged the British again'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Aspects of Experiences in World War I and Women During WWI ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Trench Warfare on the Western Front\n\nThe **Western Front** stretched from the English Channel to the Swiss border — a line of **trenches** running through Belgium and northern France.\n\n### How Trenches Developed\n\n- After the initial German advance through Belgium was stopped at the **Battle of the Marne** (September 1914), both sides dug trenches\n- The result was a **stalemate** — neither side could break through\n- Soldiers lived and fought in trenches for nearly four years\n\n### The Trench System\n\n| Part | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Front-line trench** | Closest to the enemy; where fighting took place |\n| **Support trench** | Behind the front line; reserve troops waited here |\n| **Reserve trench** | Furthest back; rest area for troops |\n| **Communication trench** | Connected the different lines; used for moving troops and supplies |\n| **No Man\'s Land** | The deadly open ground between opposing trenches |\n\n### Conditions in the Trenches\n\n- **Mud**: Trenches often flooded; soldiers stood in mud and water for days\n- **Rats**: Huge rats fed on food supplies and dead bodies\n- **Lice**: Every soldier was infested; lice spread "trench fever"\n- **Disease**: "Trench foot" (caused by standing in water) could lead to gangrene and amputation\n- **Smell**: The stench of dead bodies, unwashed soldiers, and latrines was overwhelming\n- **Cold**: Freezing winters with no adequate shelter\n- **Noise**: Constant artillery bombardment caused shell shock (now called PTSD)'),
  q(2, 'What was "No Man\'s Land"?',
    ['The deadly open ground between the opposing trenches where soldiers were most exposed to enemy fire', 'A safe area behind the trenches', 'A hospital for wounded soldiers', 'The name of a town in France'], 0,
    'No Man\'s Land was the strip of devastated ground between the front-line trenches of the opposing armies. It was covered in barbed wire, shell craters, and the bodies of fallen soldiers. Crossing it during an attack was extremely dangerous — soldiers were exposed to machine gun fire, artillery, and gas.'),
  t(3, '## New Weapons and Technology\n\nWorld War I introduced devastating new weapons that made the war more deadly than any before.\n\n### Key Weapons\n\n| Weapon | Description | Impact |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| **Machine guns** | Could fire 400-600 rounds per minute | Made attacks across No Man\'s Land suicidal |\n| **Artillery** | Heavy guns that fired explosive shells over long distances | Caused the majority of casualties; destroyed trenches and landscape |\n| **Poison gas** | Chemical weapons: chlorine, phosgene, mustard gas | Caused blindness, lung damage, painful death; introduced in 1915 |\n| **Tanks** | Armoured vehicles that could cross trenches | First used at the Battle of the Somme (1916); initially unreliable |\n| **Aircraft** | Used for reconnaissance, bombing, and fighting | Early air combat ("dogfights"); famous pilots like the Red Baron |\n| **Submarines (U-boats)** | German submarines attacked Allied shipping | Nearly starved Britain by sinking supply ships |\n| **Barbed wire** | Strung across No Man\'s Land | Trapped attacking soldiers under fire |\n| **Flame throwers** | Sprayed burning fuel at close range | Terrifying weapon used to clear trenches |\n\n### The Result\n\nThese weapons made defence much easier than attack. The combination of trenches, machine guns, barbed wire, and artillery created a **stalemate** that cost millions of lives.'),
  fb(4, 'The combination of trenches, ___ guns, and barbed wire made attacking across No Man\'s Land extremely deadly. Germany used ___ (submarines) to attack Allied shipping.',
    ['machine', 'U-boats'],
    'Machine guns could mow down hundreds of advancing soldiers in minutes. German U-boats (Unterseeboot) nearly won the war at sea by sinking supply ships heading for Britain. The introduction of convoy systems (groups of ships escorted by warships) eventually countered the U-boat threat.'),
  t(5, '## Conscription, Conscientious Objectors, and Music\n\n### Conscription\n\n- At the start of the war, volunteers rushed to join — believing the war would be over by Christmas 1914\n- As casualties mounted, voluntary enlistment was not enough\n- **Conscription** (compulsory military service) was introduced in Britain in **1916**\n- All men aged 18-41 were required to serve\n- Conscription was controversial — it forced men to fight against their will\n\n### Conscientious Objectors\n\n**Conscientious objectors** (COs) were men who refused to fight because of moral, religious, or political beliefs:\n- Some served as **stretcher-bearers** or in medical units — doing dangerous work without weapons\n- Others refused all military service and were **imprisoned**\n- COs were often seen as **cowards** and faced public hostility — they were given white feathers (a symbol of cowardice)\n- It took great courage to be a CO — they faced imprisonment, hard labour, and social rejection\n\n### Music and Poetry of the War\n\n- Soldiers composed songs to keep up morale, express feelings, and cope with horror\n- **War poets** like **Wilfred Owen** and **Siegfried Sassoon** wrote powerful poems about the reality of trench warfare\n- Owen\'s "Dulce et Decorum Est" exposed the lie that it was "sweet and fitting to die for one\'s country"\n- Propaganda songs encouraged enlistment; soldier songs often expressed disillusionment'),
  q(6, 'What was a conscientious objector during World War I?',
    ['A person who refused to fight because of moral, religious, or political beliefs', 'A soldier who fought with special enthusiasm', 'A spy who objected to the war plan', 'A general who disagreed with military strategy'], 0,
    'Conscientious objectors (COs) refused to participate in combat on moral grounds — often religious (e.g., Quakers). Some served as medics or stretcher-bearers; others refused all military service. They faced imprisonment, abuse, and the accusation of cowardice, though their stand required great moral courage.'),
  t(7, '## World War I and South Africa\n\nSouth Africa, as part of the British Empire, was automatically drawn into World War I when Britain declared war on Germany in August 1914.\n\n### South Africa\'s Role\n\n| Campaign | Detail |\n|----------|--------|\n| **German South West Africa (Namibia)** | South African forces invaded and conquered the German colony (1914-1915) |\n| **German East Africa (Tanzania)** | South African troops fought in a long, difficult campaign |\n| **Western Front** | South African troops fought in France — including the **Battle of Delville Wood** (July 1916) |\n| **Palestine** | South African forces fought against the Ottoman Empire |\n\n### The Battle of Delville Wood (July 1916)\n\n- South African soldiers fought at Delville Wood during the **Battle of the Somme**\n- Of the approximately **3 153 soldiers** who entered the wood, only **778 emerged unscathed** after six days of fighting\n- Delville Wood became known as **"Devil\'s Wood"** because of the horrific casualties\n- It remains one of the most important events in South African military history\n\n### Divisions Within South Africa\n\n- Many **Afrikaners** opposed fighting for Britain — some staged a **rebellion** in 1914 (the Maritz Rebellion)\n- **Black and Coloured South Africans** served in non-combat roles — as labourers, drivers, and stretcher-bearers\n- The **SS Mendi** sank on 21 February 1917, killing **616 black South Africans** who were being transported to France as labourers'),
  fb(8, 'South African soldiers fought at ___ Wood during the Battle of the Somme, suffering devastating casualties. The SS ___ sank in 1917, killing 616 black South African labourers.',
    ['Delville', 'Mendi'],
    'Delville Wood (called "Devil\'s Wood" by the survivors) was one of the bloodiest episodes in South African military history. The SS Mendi disaster killed 616 black South African members of the South African Native Labour Contingent — they showed remarkable bravery, singing and dancing the death dance as the ship sank.'),
  q(9, 'Why did some Afrikaners oppose South Africa\'s participation in World War I?',
    ['They did not want to fight for Britain, which had defeated the Boer republics in the South African War just 12 years earlier', 'They supported Germany because of economic ties', 'They wanted to stay neutral like Switzerland', 'All Afrikaners supported the war effort'], 0,
    'The South African War (1899-1902) was still fresh in Afrikaner memory. Many Afrikaners had lost farms, family members, and their republics to Britain. Fighting for Britain just 12 years later was unacceptable to many. Some staged a rebellion in 1914, though it was quickly suppressed.'),
  t(10, '## The Defeat of Germany and the Treaty of Versailles\n\n### End of the War\n\n- By 1918, Germany was exhausted: the British naval blockade caused food shortages; allies were collapsing; a revolution broke out\n- The USA entered the war in **April 1917** (partly because of unrestricted German submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram)\n- American troops tipped the balance — fresh soldiers and supplies for the Allies\n- On **11 November 1918** (the 11th hour of the 11th day of the 11th month), an **armistice** was signed and the fighting stopped\n\n### Women in Britain During World War I\n\nWomen\'s roles changed dramatically during the war:\n\n| Before the War | During the War |\n|----------------|----------------|\n| Limited to domestic roles and "women\'s work" | Worked in factories making weapons and ammunition ("munitionettes") |\n| Could not vote | Proved they could do "men\'s work" |\n| Few professional opportunities | Served as nurses, ambulance drivers, and clerical workers |\n| Dependent on husbands/fathers | Became financially independent |\n\n### Emmeline Pankhurst and the Vote\n\n- **Emmeline Pankhurst** led the **suffragette movement** demanding votes for women\n- Before the war, suffragettes used militant tactics (chaining themselves to railings, hunger strikes, arson)\n- During the war, Pankhurst called on women to support the war effort\n- Women\'s contribution to the war changed public attitudes\n- In **1918**, women over 30 were given the **right to vote** in Britain\n- Full equal voting rights came in **1928** (women over 21)\n\n### The Treaty of Versailles (1919)\n\n- Germany was forced to accept full blame for the war (Article 231 — the "war guilt" clause)\n- Germany had to pay massive **reparations** (£6.6 billion)\n- Germany lost territory and all its colonies\n- The German military was severely limited\n- The **League of Nations** was created to prevent future wars\n\nThe harsh terms of the Treaty of Versailles would sow the seeds of **resentment** that contributed to the rise of Hitler and World War II.'),
];

