// --- Lesson 1: Trade and Transport Around the World and in South Africa ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Trade?\n\n**Trade** is the buying and selling of goods and services. Trade can occur within a country (**domestic trade**) or between countries (**international trade**).\n\n### Why Do Countries Trade?\n\n| Reason | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Natural resources** | No country has all the resources it needs; trade allows access to what is lacking |\n| **Climate and geography** | Some goods can only be produced in certain climates (e.g., tropical fruit, wine) |\n| **Specialisation** | Countries specialise in producing what they do best (comparative advantage) |\n| **Technology** | Some countries have advanced technology for manufacturing; others provide raw materials |\n| **Consumer demand** | People want products from other countries |\n\n### Imports and Exports\n\n- **Imports**: Goods and services a country buys from other countries\n- **Exports**: Goods and services a country sells to other countries\n- **Balance of trade**: The difference between the value of exports and imports\n  - **Trade surplus**: Exports > Imports (positive balance)\n  - **Trade deficit**: Imports > Exports (negative balance)\n\n### Links Between Trade and Transport\n\nTrade depends on transport:\n- Raw materials must be moved from where they are produced to where they are processed\n- Finished goods must be transported to markets (shops, consumers)\n- The cost and efficiency of transport directly affects the cost of goods\n- Better transport infrastructure = more trade and economic growth'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between imports and exports?',
    ['Imports are goods bought from other countries, while exports are goods sold to other countries', 'Imports and exports mean the same thing', 'Imports are goods sold within a country', 'Exports are goods bought from other countries'], 0,
    'Imports are goods and services that a country buys from abroad (coming IN to the country). Exports are goods and services that a country sells to other countries (going OUT). South Africa exports minerals and imports machinery and electronics.'),
  t(3, '## Modes of Transport\n\nGoods and people are moved using different modes (types) of transport.\n\n### The Four Main Modes\n\n| Mode | Advantages | Disadvantages | Best For |\n|------|-----------|---------------|----------|\n| **Road** | Flexible; door-to-door delivery; reaches remote areas | Slow over long distances; traffic congestion; pollution | Short to medium distances; small loads |\n| **Rail** | Carries heavy loads cheaply; reliable schedule | Fixed routes; slower than road for short distances | Heavy goods (coal, iron ore); bulk transport |\n| **Sea (shipping)** | Cheapest for large volumes over long distances | Very slow; limited to ports; weather-dependent | International trade; heavy/bulky goods |\n| **Air** | Very fast; global reach | Very expensive; limited cargo capacity; high pollution | High-value goods; perishables; urgent cargo |\n\n### Pipelines\n\n- Used for transporting **liquids and gases** (oil, natural gas, water)\n- Very efficient and safe once built\n- High initial cost but low running costs\n- South Africa has pipelines for petroleum products (Durban to Johannesburg) and water (Lesotho Highlands Water Project)'),
  fb(4, 'The cheapest mode of transport for large volumes over long distances is by ___. Air transport is the fastest but also the most ___.',
    ['sea', 'expensive'],
    'Sea transport (shipping) carries about 80% of world trade by volume because it is by far the cheapest way to move large quantities of goods. Air transport is fastest but costs much more per kilogram, so it is used for high-value, lightweight, or perishable goods.'),
  t(5, '## Trade and Transport in South Africa\n\n### South Africa\'s Major Exports and Imports\n\n| Exports | Imports |\n|---------|----------|\n| Gold, platinum, diamonds | Machinery and equipment |\n| Coal | Crude oil and petroleum products |\n| Iron ore and other minerals | Electronics |\n| Vehicles and vehicle parts | Chemicals |\n| Fruit, wine, and agricultural products | Manufactured goods |\n\n### Major Trading Partners\n\n| Partner | Relationship |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **China** | Largest trading partner; buys SA minerals; sells manufactured goods |\n| **Germany** | Major trade in vehicles and machinery |\n| **USA** | Important market for SA vehicles, minerals, and agricultural products |\n| **India** | Growing trade in minerals and manufactured goods |\n| **UK** | Historical trading partner; fruit, wine, minerals |\n| **African countries** | South Africa is Africa\'s largest economy; trades manufactured goods for raw materials |\n\n### Major Transport Routes in South Africa\n\n| Route | Significance |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Durban harbour** | Africa\'s busiest port; handles most of SA\'s imports and exports |\n| **Richards Bay** | Major coal export terminal |\n| **Saldanha Bay** | Iron ore export terminal |\n| **Cape Town harbour** | Container terminal; connects to European and American markets |\n| **N1, N2, N3 highways** | National routes connecting major cities |\n| **Railway network** | Transnet Freight Rail moves coal, iron ore, and general cargo |'),
  q(6, 'Why is Durban harbour the most important port in South Africa?',
    ['It is Africa\'s busiest port, handling the majority of South Africa\'s imports and exports due to its location and capacity', 'It is the only port in South Africa', 'It only handles passenger ships', 'It is the smallest but most modern port'], 0,
    'Durban harbour handles more cargo than any other port in Africa. Its strategic location on the Indian Ocean coast makes it ideal for trade with Asia (particularly China and India). The harbour can handle container ships, bulk carriers, and tankers.'),
  t(7, '## South African Harbours and Shipping\n\n### Case Study: A South African Harbour\n\n**Durban Harbour (Port of Durban)**\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Location** | East coast of KwaZulu-Natal; natural bay |\n| **Significance** | Africa\'s busiest port; handles 60%+ of SA\'s container traffic |\n| **Reasons for location** | Natural deep harbour; railway connections to Johannesburg and interior; closest major port to the Witwatersrand |\n| **Main exports** | Minerals, sugar, citrus, vehicles |\n| **Main imports** | Crude oil, machinery, electronics, consumer goods |\n| **Specialised ships** | Container ships, tankers, bulk carriers, car carriers |\n\n### Types of Specialised Ships\n\n| Ship Type | Cargo |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Container ships** | Standard containers (TEUs) carrying manufactured goods |\n| **Tankers** | Crude oil and petroleum products |\n| **Bulk carriers** | Coal, iron ore, grain — loose bulk cargo |\n| **Car carriers** | Vehicles transported on multiple decks |\n| **Passenger liners** | Cruise ships carrying tourists |\n\n### Links to Other Transport Systems\n\n- Harbours are connected to the interior by **railways** and **roads**\n- A harbour without good inland connections is useless\n- The railway from Durban to Johannesburg was one of the first major lines built in South Africa\n- **Pipelines** from Durban carry petroleum products to Gauteng'),
  fb(8, 'Durban handles over 60% of South Africa\'s ___ traffic. Ships that carry standardised cargo boxes are called ___ ships.',
    ['container', 'container'],
    'Containerisation revolutionised global shipping. Standard-sized containers (TEUs — Twenty-foot Equivalent Units) can be loaded onto ships, trains, and trucks efficiently. Durban is South Africa\'s primary container port, handling the majority of the country\'s containerised trade.'),
  q(9, 'Why was a railway built from Durban to Johannesburg?',
    ['To transport goods between South Africa\'s busiest port and its largest economic centre on the Witwatersrand', 'For tourism only', 'To move people to the beach', 'Durban had no use for a railway'], 0,
    'The railway from Durban to Johannesburg was essential for transporting imported goods from the port to the goldfields and industrial centres of the Witwatersrand, and for exporting gold, minerals, and other goods from the interior to the port. This remains one of South Africa\'s most important transport corridors.'),
  t(10, '## Requirements for Future Transport Networks\n\nSouth Africa\'s transport infrastructure needs significant investment and improvement to support economic growth.\n\n### Current Challenges\n\n| Challenge | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Road deterioration** | Many national and provincial roads are in poor condition; potholes damage vehicles |\n| **Rail decline** | Freight and passenger rail services have declined; theft of infrastructure (cables, rails) |\n| **Port congestion** | Durban and other ports face congestion and inefficiency |\n| **Rural access** | Many rural areas have poor roads and no public transport |\n| **Safety** | High rates of road accidents; South Africa has one of the highest road death rates in the world |\n\n### Future Requirements\n\n- **Maintain existing infrastructure**: Fix roads, railways, and ports\n- **Invest in rail**: Rail is cheaper and cleaner than road for heavy freight\n- **Improve public transport**: BRT systems, commuter rail, and better minibus taxi regulation\n- **Green transport**: Electric vehicles, improved fuel efficiency, non-motorised transport (cycling, walking)\n- **Rural connectivity**: All-weather roads to connect rural communities to services and markets\n- **Technology**: Smart traffic management, logistics optimisation, and digital freight matching\n\nTransport infrastructure is essential for economic development. Without efficient, affordable transport, trade cannot flourish, businesses cannot grow, and people cannot access jobs and services.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: People and Transport in Urban Areas ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Public Transport in South African Cities\n\nMillions of South Africans rely on public transport to get to work, school, and other destinations every day.\n\n### Modes of Urban Transport\n\n| Mode | Description | Users |\n|------|-------------|-------|\n| **Minibus taxis** | 16-seater minibuses; the most widely used form of public transport | Approximately 15 million trips per day |\n| **Metrorail** | Commuter trains in major cities (Johannesburg, Cape Town, Durban, Pretoria) | Working-class commuters |\n| **Buses** | Municipal buses (e.g., Golden Arrow in Cape Town) and BRT systems | Medium-distance commuters |\n| **BRT (Bus Rapid Transit)** | Modern bus systems with dedicated lanes — Rea Vaya (JHB), MyCiTi (CPT) | Growing number of commuters |\n| **Gautrain** | Modern rapid rail linking Johannesburg, Pretoria, and OR Tambo Airport | Middle-class commuters and travellers |\n| **Private cars** | Personal vehicles | Middle and upper income |\n| **Walking** | On foot | Many low-income residents walk long distances |\n| **Cycling** | Bicycles | Limited but growing |'),
  q(2, 'What is the most widely used form of public transport in South Africa?',
    ['Minibus taxis, carrying approximately 15 million trips per day', 'Metrorail trains', 'The Gautrain', 'Private cars'], 0,
    'Minibus taxis dominate South African public transport, carrying an estimated 15 million passenger trips daily. They serve routes that formal public transport does not cover and are essential for connecting townships to economic centres.'),
  t(3, '## Transport Issues in Urban Areas\n\nUrban transport in South Africa faces serious challenges that affect millions of people daily.\n\n### Traffic Congestion\n\n- Johannesburg, Cape Town, and Durban experience severe **traffic congestion** during peak hours\n- Causes: too many private cars, inadequate public transport, poorly planned road networks\n- Effects: wasted time, wasted fuel, air pollution, stress, economic losses\n\n### Cost of Transport\n\n- Many South Africans spend **20-40% of their income** on transport\n- This is much higher than the international benchmark of 10%\n- The apartheid legacy means many people live far from their workplaces\n- Transport costs reduce disposable income and trap people in poverty\n\n### Safety\n\n| Issue | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| **Road accidents** | South Africa has one of the highest road death rates in the world — over 12 000 deaths per year |\n| **Minibus taxi safety** | Overloading, unroadworthy vehicles, reckless driving |\n| **Pedestrian safety** | Many pedestrians are killed because roads lack sidewalks and crossings |\n| **Crime** | Commuters face risks of robbery, especially at train stations and taxi ranks |\n| **Train safety** | Metrorail services have declined; overcrowding, delays, and vandalism |'),
  fb(4, 'Many South Africans spend 20-40% of their income on ___, far higher than the international benchmark of 10%. South Africa has over ___ road deaths per year.',
    ['transport', '12 000'],
    'The high cost of transport is a direct legacy of apartheid spatial planning, which placed townships far from economic centres. South Africa\'s road death toll is one of the highest in the world, driven by speeding, drunk driving, pedestrian vulnerability, and poor road conditions.'),
  t(5, '## Private Transport\n\n### The Growth of Private Car Ownership\n\n- South Africa has approximately **12 million registered vehicles**\n- Car ownership is growing, especially among the emerging middle class\n- Most cars carry only **one person** (the driver) during rush hour\n\n### Advantages of Private Cars\n\n- Convenience and flexibility — go where you want, when you want\n- Door-to-door transport\n- Comfortable and private\n\n### Disadvantages of Private Cars\n\n| Problem | Impact |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Congestion** | More cars = more traffic; gridlock during peak hours |\n| **Pollution** | Cars produce CO₂, nitrogen oxides, and particulate matter |\n| **Cost** | Fuel, insurance, maintenance, tolls — expensive |\n| **Space** | Parking lots and wide roads take up valuable urban land |\n| **Accidents** | Private vehicles are involved in most road accidents |\n| **Inequality** | Only the relatively wealthy can afford cars; most South Africans cannot |\n\n### Alternatives to Encourage\n\n- **Carpooling**: Sharing rides reduces the number of cars on the road\n- **Park and ride**: Drive to a station, then use public transport\n- **Cycling**: Healthy, cheap, and non-polluting\n- **Walking**: For short distances, the most sustainable option'),
  q(6, 'Why is the increasing number of private cars a problem for South African cities?',
    ['More cars cause worse traffic congestion, more pollution, and require expensive road infrastructure while most South Africans cannot afford cars', 'Private cars reduce pollution', 'More cars mean less traffic', 'Private cars are the most efficient form of transport'], 0,
    'Private cars are inefficient in urban areas — one person occupying a vehicle designed for five, taking up road space and creating congestion. They produce air pollution, require expensive parking, and widen inequality since most South Africans rely on public transport.'),
  t(7, '## Public Transit Strategies\n\nCities around the world — and in South Africa — are developing public transit solutions to address transport challenges.\n\n### Bus Rapid Transit (BRT)\n\n- BRT systems use **dedicated bus lanes** so buses are not stuck in traffic\n- **Rea Vaya** (Johannesburg) and **MyCiTi** (Cape Town) are South Africa\'s BRT systems\n- Features: modern buses, enclosed stations, prepaid smart cards, reliable schedules\n- BRT is much cheaper to build than rail systems\n\n### Integrated Transport\n\nModern cities aim for **integrated transport systems** where different modes connect seamlessly:\n- A commuter might walk to a bus stop, take a BRT to a train station, and then take a train to the CBD\n- **Single ticketing**: One card works on buses, trains, and BRT\n- **Timetable coordination**: Connections are timed so passengers don\'t wait long\n\n### Subsidised Public Transport\n\n- Government **subsidies** keep public transport affordable for low-income commuters\n- Without subsidies, many South Africans would not be able to afford to get to work\n- The challenge is balancing affordability with the cost of providing services\n\n### Car-Free Zones\n\nSome cities create **car-free zones** in central areas:\n- Reduces pollution and congestion in the CBD\n- Makes the area more pleasant for pedestrians and cyclists\n- Encourages public transport use\n- Examples worldwide: Amsterdam, Bogota; South Africa is experimenting with restricted zones'),
  fb(8, 'BRT stands for Bus ___ Transit. South Africa\'s BRT systems include Rea Vaya in Johannesburg and ___ in Cape Town.',
    ['Rapid', 'MyCiTi'],
    'BRT systems provide fast, reliable bus services using dedicated lanes that keep buses out of traffic. Rea Vaya and MyCiTi have modernised public transport in their cities, though both still need expansion to serve more communities.'),
  q(9, 'What is an integrated transport system?',
    ['A system where different modes of transport (buses, trains, BRT) connect seamlessly, allowing passengers to transfer easily between them', 'A system where only one type of transport exists', 'A system with no public transport', 'A system designed only for private cars'], 0,
    'An integrated transport system coordinates different modes of transport so passengers can move seamlessly between them. This includes coordinated timetables, connected routes, and single-payment systems. It makes public transport more convenient and competitive with private cars.'),
  t(10, '## Sustainable Transport for the Future\n\nSouth Africa must develop sustainable transport systems that are affordable, accessible, and environmentally friendly.\n\n### Key Principles\n\n| Principle | Application |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Accessibility** | Transport must be available to all — including people in rural areas, people with disabilities, and the poor |\n| **Affordability** | Transport costs should not exceed 10% of household income |\n| **Efficiency** | Move more people with less energy and fewer vehicles |\n| **Safety** | Reduce road deaths through better roads, driver education, and enforcement |\n| **Environmental sustainability** | Reduce pollution and carbon emissions |\n\n### Future Technologies\n\n- **Electric vehicles (EVs)**: Produce no emissions at the tailpipe; battery costs are falling\n- **Solar-powered transport**: South Africa\'s abundant sunshine makes solar-charged EVs viable\n- **Smart traffic management**: Using technology to optimise traffic flow and reduce congestion\n- **Non-motorised transport**: Dedicated cycling lanes and pedestrian walkways\n- **E-hailing**: Services like Uber and Bolt provide flexible transport options\n\n### The Transport Challenge in Context\n\nTransport connects people to jobs, services, and opportunities. For South Africa, fixing transport means:\n- Addressing the spatial legacy of apartheid\n- Investing in public transport infrastructure\n- Making cities more compact and walkable\n- Creating transport systems that serve all South Africans, not just car owners\n\nGood transport is not just about moving people — it is about building a more equal and connected society.'),
];

